QB.add(2,[{"id":2001,"t":"Autonomic Nervous System","s":"Which statement best defines the autonomic nervous system (ANS) and its roles?","o":["The involuntary part of the PNS that regulates internal organs and the internal environment","The voluntary part of the PNS that regulates internal organs and the internal environment","The involuntary part of the CNS that regulates internal organs and the internal environment","The involuntary part of the PNS that controls contraction of skeletal muscle","The involuntary part of the PNS that carries only sensory information to the CNS"],"e":"The ANS is the <b>involuntary</b> aspect of the <b>peripheral</b> nervous system. It <b>regulates operation of the internal organs</b>, allowing <b>optimisation of performance</b>, and <b>maintains the internal environment</b>. Its two main divisions are sympathetic and parasympathetic.","w":["The ANS is involuntary: we do not have to think about it.","The ANS is part of the peripheral nervous system.","Skeletal muscle contraction is the somatic (voluntary) system.","The ANS is mainly efferent to organs; it is not purely sensory."],"k":"ANS = involuntary PNS: regulates organs, maintains internal environment.","rn":[["","Autonomic Nervous System","Autonomic Nervous System","Involuntary aspect of the PNS; Regulates internal organs; Maintains internal environment"],["","What are the 2 roles of involuntary control?","What are the 2 roles of involuntary control?","Regulates internal organs; Maintains internal environment"]]},{"id":2002,"t":"Autonomic Nervous System","s":"A student is about to give a presentation and notices a “funny feeling” in their stomach. Which component of the sympathetic response best explains this?","o":["Constriction of vessels to the GI tract","Increased digestion in the GI tract","Increased salivation","Dilation of vessels to the GI tract","Constriction of the airways"],"e":"Feeling nervous before a talk triggers part of the sympathetic response: <b>constriction of blood vessels to non-essential organs</b> such as the <b>GI tract</b>, giving a funny sensation in the stomach.","w":["Digestion increases with the parasympathetic (rest and digest) division.","Salivation is a parasympathetic effect.","Sympathetic activity constricts vessels to non-essential organs such as the GI tract.","Sympathetic activity dilates the airways."],"k":"Sympathetic: reduced blood flow to non-essential organs (GI tract, kidneys).","rn":[["","Vessels to which organs constrict in fight or flight?","Vessels to which organs constrict in fight or flight?","Non-essential organs; Kidneys; GI tract"],["","What causes butterflies in your stomach before public speaking?","What causes butterflies in your stomach before public speaking?","Sympathetic response; Constriction of blood flow to the GI tract"]]},{"id":2003,"t":"Autonomic Nervous System","s":"A hiker suddenly sees a bear. Which set of changes would you expect?","o":["Dilated pupils, dilated airways, increased heart rate","Constricted pupils, dilated airways, increased heart rate","Dilated pupils, constricted airways, increased heart rate","Dilated pupils, dilated airways, decreased heart rate","Constricted pupils, constricted airways, decreased heart rate"],"e":"This is a <b>sympathetic (fight or flight)</b> response: <b>dilation of pupils and airways</b>, <b>increased cardiac and respiratory functioning</b>, dilation of vessels to muscles, <b>mobilisation of glucose and fats</b> and increased metabolism.","w":["Sympathetic activity dilates the pupils.","Sympathetic activity dilates the airways.","Heart rate increases in fight or flight.","This is the parasympathetic (rest and digest) pattern."],"k":"Fight or flight: dilated pupils/airways, ↑HR, mobilised nutrients.","rn":[["","What does the sympathetic division do to the airways?","What does the sympathetic division do to the airways?","Dilates them; Respiratory rate increases"]]},{"id":2004,"t":"Autonomic Nervous System","s":"To which tissues does sympathetic activation <b>dilate</b> blood vessels?","o":["Skeletal muscle, cardiac muscle, liver and adipose tissue","Skeletal muscle, cardiac muscle, kidneys and GI tract","Skeletal muscle, liver, kidneys and salivary glands","Cardiac muscle, adipose tissue, GI tract and bladder","Kidneys, GI tract, liver and adipose tissue"],"e":"Sympathetic effects include <b>dilation of vessels to muscles, cardiac muscle, liver and adipose tissue</b>, and <b>constriction</b> of vessels to non-essential organs such as the <b>kidneys and GI tract</b>.","w":["Kidneys and GI tract are non-essential organs whose vessels constrict.","Kidney vessels constrict in the sympathetic response.","GI vessels constrict in the sympathetic response.","Kidneys and GI tract vessels constrict."],"k":"Sympathetic dilates vessels to muscle, heart, liver, adipose; constricts to kidneys, GI.","rn":[["","Vessels to which 4 tissues dilate in fight or flight?","Vessels to which 4 tissues dilate in fight or flight?","Muscles; Cardiac muscle; Liver; Adipose tissue"],["","What are the 7 sympathetic effects?","What are the 7 sympathetic effects?","Mobilise nutrients; Dilate pupils; Increase cardiac and respiratory functioning; Dilate airways; Increase metabolism; Constrict vessels to non-essential organs; Dilate vessels to muscles, heart muscle, liver and adipose tissue; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":2005,"t":"Autonomic Nervous System","s":"Which of the following is a parasympathetic effect?","o":["Lacrimation","Dilation of the airways","Mobilisation of fats","Increased metabolism","Dilation of the pupils"],"e":"Parasympathetic “rest and digest” effects: <b>salivation, lacrimation (crying), urination, digestion, defecation</b>, and <b>decreased</b> HR, airway diameter and pupil diameter (SLUDD).","w":["Sympathetic.","Sympathetic.","Sympathetic.","Sympathetic."],"k":"SLUDD: salivation, lacrimation, urination, digestion, defecation.","rn":[["","What are the 6 parasympathetic effects?","What are the 6 parasympathetic effects?","Salivation; Lacrimation; Urination; Digestion; Defecation; Decreased HR, airway diameter, pupil diameter; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":2006,"t":"Autonomic Nervous System","s":"After a large meal, a person relaxes on the sofa. Which change in the eye and airways would be expected?","o":["Decreased pupil diameter and decreased airway diameter","Increased pupil diameter and increased airway diameter","Increased pupil diameter and decreased airway diameter","Decreased pupil diameter and increased airway diameter","No change in either"],"e":"At rest the <b>parasympathetic</b> division dominates: <b>decreased</b> HR, <b>airway diameter</b> and <b>pupil diameter</b>.","w":["Sympathetic.","Mixed.","Mixed.","The ANS adjusts both."],"k":"Parasympathetic: ↓HR, ↓airway diameter, ↓pupil diameter.","rn":[["","What are the 6 parasympathetic effects?","What are the 6 parasympathetic effects?","Salivation; Lacrimation; Urination; Digestion; Defecation; Decreased HR, airway diameter, pupil diameter; MNEMONIC"],["","Compare sympathetic vs parasympathetic effect on the airways","Compare sympathetic vs parasympathetic effect on the airways","Sympathetic: dilates; Parasympathetic: decreases diameter"],["","Compare sympathetic vs parasympathetic effect on the pupils","Compare sympathetic vs parasympathetic effect on the pupils","Sympathetic: dilates; Parasympathetic: decreases diameter"]]},{"id":2007,"t":"Autonomic Nervous System","s":"How do the two divisions of the ANS differ in their use of metabolic resources?","o":["Sympathetic maximises use of resources; parasympathetic conserves them","Sympathetic conserves resources; parasympathetic maximises their use","Both divisions maximise the use of metabolic resources","Both divisions conserve resources for future use","Sympathetic conserves resources; parasympathetic has no effect on them"],"e":"<b>Sympathetic</b> (fight or flight) <b>maximises use</b> of metabolic resources; <b>parasympathetic</b> (rest and digest) <b>increases/conserves</b> them, laying down resources for future use.","w":["Reversed.","Parasympathetic conserves/lays down resources.","Sympathetic maximises use of resources.","Parasympathetic increases/conserves resources."],"k":"Sympathetic spends; parasympathetic saves.","rn":[["","Sympathetic Function","Sympathetic Function","Fight or flight response; Maximises use of metabolic resources"]]},{"id":2008,"t":"Autonomic Nervous System","s":"Why can the sympathetic division affect many organs at the same time, whereas the parasympathetic division cannot?","o":["Sympathetic nerves lie near the vertebral column and branch plentifully","Sympathetic nerves lie near target organs and branch plentifully","Sympathetic nerves lie near the vertebral column and branch very little","Sympathetic nerves arise from the brainstem and branch plentifully","Sympathetic nerves use a single neuron to every organ they supply"],"e":"Sympathetic nerves are located <b>close to the vertebral column</b> with <b>plentiful branching</b>, so <b>multiple organs are innervated simultaneously</b>. Parasympathetic nerves are <b>close to target organs</b> with <b>very little branching</b>, so they are unable to affect multiple organs rapidly.","w":["Parasympathetic nerves are the ones near target organs.","Very little branching describes the parasympathetic division.","Sympathetic outflow is thoracolumbar.","Only the adrenal medulla has a single-axon supply."],"k":"Sympathetic: near spine, lots of branching → many organs at once.","rn":[["","Where are parasympathetic nerves located?","Where are parasympathetic nerves located?","Close to the vertebral column"],["","Why can the sympathetic division act on multiple organs at once?","Why can the sympathetic division act on multiple organs at once?","Plentiful nerve branching; Multiple organs innervated simultaneously"]]},{"id":2009,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","s":"Which division of the ANS originates from the thoracic and lumbar spinal cord?","e":"Sympathetic: thoracolumbar outflow. Parasympathetic: craniosacral (brainstem and sacral cord).","k":"Sympathetic.","a":["sympathetic","sympathetic division","sympathetic nervous system","thoracolumbar"],"rn":[["","What are the 3 origins of the ANS?","What are the 3 origins of the ANS?","Parasympathetic: brainstem; Sympathetic: thoracic/lumbar spinal cord; Parasympathetic: sacral spinal cord"]]},{"id":2010,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","f":"nt10","s":"On a map of the ANS, which two regions give rise to parasympathetic fibres?","o":["Brainstem and sacral spinal cord","Thoracic and lumbar spinal cord","Cervical and sacral spinal cord","Brainstem and thoracic spinal cord","Lumbar and sacral spinal cord"],"e":"Parasympathetic fibres come from the <b>brainstem</b> (via cranial nerves) and the <b>sacral spinal cord</b>: <b>craniosacral</b>. Memory aid: “para goes to the ends: head and tail”.","w":["Sympathetic.","Not correct.","Thoracic is sympathetic.","Lumbar is sympathetic."],"k":"Parasympathetic = brainstem + sacral.","rn":[["","What are the 3 origins of the ANS?","What are the 3 origins of the ANS?","Parasympathetic: brainstem; Sympathetic: thoracic/lumbar spinal cord; Parasympathetic: sacral spinal cord"],["","What are the 2 parasympathetic pathways?","What are the 2 parasympathetic pathways?","Brainstem via cranial nerves; Sacral spinal cord via pelvic splanchnic nerves"]]},{"id":2012,"t":"Autonomic Nervous System","s":"Two people walk past the same large dog. One has a strong fight-or-flight response; the other does not. Which explanation best accounts for this?","o":["A threat is individual, depending on appraisal, resources and experience","A threat is universal, but one person has a faster sympathetic response","A threat is universal, but one person has a slower parasympathetic response","A threat is individual, depending only on the size of the adrenal medulla","A threat is detected by the cerebellum, which differs between people"],"e":"<b>What counts as a threat is individual</b>: it depends on your <b>primary appraisal</b> of your surroundings and resources, and your <b>background and experiences</b>. So we should not minimise someone's stress response.","w":["What counts as a threat is individual, not universal.","Speed differences between divisions do not explain this.","The explanation was primary appraisal, resources, background and experiences.","Threat appraisal was not attributed to the cerebellum."],"k":"Threat perception is individual (primary appraisal, experience).","rn":[["","Is fight or flight about an imminent threat?","Is fight or flight about an imminent threat?","No, the perception of threat; It is an individual experience"],["","What influences the perception of threat?","What influences the perception of threat?","Primary appraisal of your surroundings; Internal and external resources; Your background and experiences; The lens through which you view the world"]]},{"id":2013,"t":"Autonomic Nervous System","s":"Name the structure next to the hippocampus that detects threats and triggers the stress response.","e":"The amygdala, lying next to the hippocampus, detects threats.","k":"Amygdala.","a":["amygdala","amygdalae"],"rn":[["","Which system is the amygdala part of?","Which system is the amygdala part of?","Limbic system"],["","Where is the amygdala?","Where is the amygdala?","Rostral medial tip of the hippocampus"],["","Which structure is responsible for threat detection?","Which structure is responsible for threat detection?","Amygdala (amygdaloid complex)"]]},{"id":2014,"t":"Autonomic Nervous System","s":"Which statement about the relationship between the sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions is most accurate?","o":["In reality there is an interplay between the two divisions","Only one division can be active in any organ at a time","The parasympathetic division is active only during sleep","The sympathetic division is active only during exercise","The two divisions never supply the same organ"],"e":"The lecturer emphasised that in reality there is an <b>interplay</b> between the two divisions; sexual function is a nice example of them working together.","w":["They work together, e.g. sexual function.","The parasympathetic division acts at rest generally, not only in sleep.","Sympathetic responses occur with any perceived threat.","Many organs (e.g. heart) receive both."],"k":"Sympathetic and parasympathetic work in interplay."},{"id":2015,"t":"Autonomic Nervous System","s":"Which statement about the speed of response of the two ANS divisions is correct?","o":["Sympathetic responses are rapid; parasympathetic responses are slower","Parasympathetic responses are rapid; sympathetic responses are slower","Both divisions respond equally rapidly","Both divisions respond equally slowly","Sympathetic responses are rapid only in the adrenal medulla"],"e":"The sympathetic response needs to be <b>quick</b> because survival depends on it. The parasympathetic is a <b>much slower process</b>.","w":["Reversed.","The parasympathetic is a much slower process.","The sympathetic needs to be quick because survival depends on it.","The sympathetic response is rapid generally."],"k":"Sympathetic rapid; parasympathetic slower.","rn":[["","Why does the parasympathetic division not need to be fast?","Why does the parasympathetic division not need to be fast?","We are not fighting for survival; The sympathetic division handles rapid responses"],["","Which division acts fast and which acts slowly?","Which division acts fast and which acts slowly?","Sympathetic: rapid; Parasympathetic: much slower process"]]},{"id":2016,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","f":"nt11","s":"In a typical autonomic pathway, what is the correct sequence from the CNS to the effector?","o":["Preganglionic neuron → autonomic ganglion → postganglionic neuron → effector","Postganglionic neuron → autonomic ganglion → preganglionic neuron → effector","Preganglionic neuron → postganglionic neuron → autonomic ganglion → effector","Preganglionic neuron → effector → autonomic ganglion → postganglionic neuron","Autonomic ganglion → preganglionic neuron → postganglionic neuron → effector"],"e":"The ANS <b>usually</b> uses <b>two neurons</b>: the <b>preganglionic neuron</b> runs from the CNS to an <b>autonomic ganglion</b>, synapses there (neurotransmitter <b>acetylcholine</b>), and the <b>postganglionic neuron</b> runs to the <b>effector</b>.","w":["Reversed order of the neurons.","The synapse in the ganglion is between pre- and postganglionic neurons.","The effector is the final target.","The pathway starts with the preganglionic neuron in the CNS."],"k":"Pre → ganglion (ACh) → post → effector.","rn":[["","What is the order of the two-neuron chain?","What is the order of the two-neuron chain?","Preganglionic neuron; Synapse in ganglion (acetylcholine); Postganglionic neuron; Effector organ"],["","Preganglionic Neuron","Preganglionic Neuron","From the CNS to an autonomic ganglion"],["","Postganglionic Neuron","Postganglionic Neuron","From the ganglion to the effector organ"]]},{"id":2017,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","s":"Name the neurotransmitter released at the synapse inside an autonomic ganglion.","e":"Preganglionic neurons release acetylcholine onto postganglionic neurons in autonomic ganglia.","k":"Acetylcholine.","a":["acetylcholine","ach"],"rn":[["","What is the neurotransmitter at the autonomic ganglion?","What is the neurotransmitter at the autonomic ganglion?","Acetylcholine"],["","What is the order of the two-neuron chain?","What is the order of the two-neuron chain?","Preganglionic neuron; Synapse in ganglion (acetylcholine); Postganglionic neuron; Effector organ"]]},{"id":2018,"t":"Somatic Nervous System","s":"Which tissue is <b>not</b> an effector of the autonomic nervous system?","o":["Skeletal muscle","Smooth muscle","Cardiac muscle","Glands","Smooth muscle of blood vessels"],"e":"The postganglionic neuron supplies <b>smooth muscle, cardiac muscle or glands</b>. <b>Skeletal muscle</b> is supplied by the <b>somatic</b> system via a single motor neuron.","w":["An ANS effector.","An ANS effector.","An ANS effector.","Smooth muscle is an ANS effector."],"k":"ANS effectors: smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, glands.","rn":[["","Somatic vs autonomic: effector","Somatic vs autonomic: effector","Somatic: skeletal muscle; Autonomic: smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, glands"],["","What are the 3 effector organs of the ANS?","What are the 3 effector organs of the ANS?","Smooth muscle; Cardiac muscle; Glands"]]},{"id":2019,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","s":"Name the structure that receives sympathetic supply from a single preganglionic axon, with no postganglionic neuron.","e":"The adrenal medulla is supplied directly by preganglionic sympathetic axons and releases adrenaline and noradrenaline into the blood.","k":"Adrenal medulla.","a":["adrenal medulla","adrenal","adrenal gland","suprarenal medulla"],"rn":[["","How is the adrenal medulla supplied?","How is the adrenal medulla supplied?","A single preganglionic axon only; Monosynaptic pathway"],["","What does the adrenal medulla release?","What does the adrenal medulla release?","Adrenaline; Noradrenaline"],["","What is the exception to the two-neuron rule?","What is the exception to the two-neuron rule?","Sympathetic supply to the adrenal medulla"]]},{"id":2020,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","s":"Why is a single-axon sympathetic supply to the adrenal medulla advantageous?","o":["With no second synapse the pathway is quicker, suiting fight or flight","With no second synapse the pathway conserves metabolic resources","With no second synapse the medulla releases acetylcholine into blood","With no second synapse the parasympathetic division can control it","With no second synapse adrenaline release is prevented during stress"],"e":"Because there is <b>only one axon</b> (no second synapse), the pathway is <b>quicker</b>, which in evolutionary terms is <b>advantageous</b>. The adrenal medulla releases <b>adrenaline and noradrenaline</b>, one of the initial parts of fight or flight.","w":["Conserving resources is parasympathetic; the advantage was speed.","The medulla releases adrenaline and noradrenaline.","The supply is sympathetic.","It promotes adrenaline release in fight or flight."],"k":"One axon → quicker adrenal response.","rn":[["","Why is a single axon to the adrenal medulla advantageous?","Why is a single axon to the adrenal medulla advantageous?","Quicker; Cuts out the second synapse; Milliseconds matter for survival"]]},{"id":2021,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","s":"What does the adrenal medulla release when stimulated by its sympathetic supply?","o":["Adrenaline and noradrenaline","Acetylcholine and noradrenaline","Acetylcholine only","Adrenaline and acetylcholine","Noradrenaline only"],"e":"The <b>adrenal medulla</b> (adrenal glands sit above the kidneys) releases <b>adrenaline and noradrenaline</b>.","w":["Acetylcholine is the ganglionic transmitter, not the medullary hormone.","The medulla releases adrenaline and noradrenaline.","Acetylcholine is released by the preganglionic axon, not the medulla.","It releases both adrenaline and noradrenaline."],"k":"Adrenal medulla: adrenaline + noradrenaline.","rn":[["","What does the adrenal medulla release?","What does the adrenal medulla release?","Adrenaline; Noradrenaline"]]},{"id":2022,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","s":"Why is the classic two-neuron autonomic chain a simplification? What does it leave out?","o":["Interneurons, which sit between neurons and regulate them","A third ganglion found in every autonomic pathway","Glial cells, which conduct impulses between neurons","Sensory neurons, which replace the preganglionic neuron","A direct connection to skeletal muscle"],"e":"The two-neuron chain is a <b>simplification</b>: most networks also contain <b>interneurons</b>, which sit between neurons and regulate them.","w":["No third ganglion was described.","Glia are non-excitable.","Not what was said; interneurons were missing.","Skeletal muscle is somatic."],"k":"Real networks include interneurons.","rn":[["","Interneurons","Interneurons","Sit between neurons; Regulate them; Like a brake; Mostly inhibitory"],["","Is the two-neuron chain the full picture?","Is the two-neuron chain the full picture?","No, a simplification; Most networks also contain interneurons"]]},{"id":2023,"t":"Spinal Nerves","s":"What is an autonomic ganglion?","o":["A cluster of nerve cell bodies where pre- and postganglionic neurons synapse","A cluster of nerve cell bodies on the dorsal root carrying sensory input","A bundle of myelinated axons running from the cord to the organ","The opening through which a spinal nerve leaves the vertebral column","The gap between two segments of myelin along an autonomic axon"],"e":"<b>Autonomic ganglia</b> are where <b>preganglionic neurons synapse with postganglionic neurons</b>. A ganglion is a structure containing several nerve cell bodies.","w":["That is the dorsal root ganglion (sensory), not an autonomic ganglion.","A ganglion contains cell bodies, not just axons.","That is the intervertebral foramen.","That is a node of Ranvier."],"k":"Autonomic ganglion: pre–post synapse; cluster of cell bodies.","rn":[["","Dorsal Root Ganglion","Dorsal Root Ganglion","A cluster of cell bodies; On the dorsal root"],["","Ganglia","Ganglia","A structure containing several nerve cell bodies"],["","Autonomic Ganglia","Autonomic Ganglia","Where preganglionic neurons synapse with postganglionic neurons"]]},{"id":2024,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","f":"nt12","s":"Which statement about the sympathetic trunk is correct?","o":["A chain of paravertebral ganglia along the length of the vertebral column","A chain of prevertebral ganglia along the thoracic spine only","A chain of parasympathetic ganglia close to the target organs","A chain of paravertebral ganglia that only some preganglionic axons enter","A single large ganglion lying above the adrenal gland"],"e":"The sympathetic trunk is made of <b>interconnected paravertebral ganglia</b>, <b>extends the length of the vertebral column</b>, and <b>all preganglionic sympathetic axons enter it</b>. It is a major component of the sympathetic system.","w":["The trunk is paravertebral and extends the whole column.","Parasympathetic ganglia are near organs, not in the trunk.","All preganglionic sympathetic axons enter it.","It is a chain of interconnected ganglia."],"k":"Sympathetic trunk: paravertebral ganglia, whole spine, all pre-sympathetic axons enter.","rn":[["","Sympathetic Trunk","Sympathetic Trunk","Interconnected paravertebral ganglia; Extends the length of the vertebral column; Major component of the sympathetic NS"]]},{"id":2025,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","s":"A preganglionic sympathetic axon leaves the spinal cord at T5 but needs to supply a structure far above that level. How is this possible?","o":["It enters the sympathetic trunk and ascends to synapse at a higher level","It enters the sympathetic trunk and synapses only at the T5 level","It crosses into the brainstem to join the parasympathetic outflow","It runs directly to a ganglion inside the target organ","It joins the pelvic splanchnic nerves to travel upwards"],"e":"Once in the trunk (the “motorway”), a preganglionic axon can <b>synapse at the same level</b> or travel <b>up or down</b> to synapse at a <b>higher or lower level</b>. This lets the sympathetic system reach structures far from where the axon left the cord.","w":["Axons can travel up or down the trunk to synapse at other levels.","Parasympathetic outflow is cranial/sacral.","Ganglia in or near organs are parasympathetic.","Pelvic splanchnics are sacral parasympathetic."],"k":"In the trunk, axons can synapse at the same, a higher or a lower level.","rn":[["","What are the 3 things a sympathetic axon can do in the trunk?","What are the 3 things a sympathetic axon can do in the trunk?","Synapse at the same level; Ascend and synapse at a higher level; Descend and synapse at a lower level"]]},{"id":2026,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","s":"The “gangliated cord” running alongside the vertebral column is better known as what?","e":"The sympathetic trunk (gangliated cord) is a chain of paravertebral ganglia; preganglionic axons can ascend or descend in it to synapse at other levels.","k":"Sympathetic trunk.","a":["sympathetic trunk","sympathetic chain","paravertebral chain","sympathetic ganglia chain"],"rn":[["","What are the 3 names for the sympathetic trunk?","What are the 3 names for the sympathetic trunk?","Sympathetic trunk; Sympathetic cord; Gangliated cord"],["","What are the 3 things a sympathetic axon can do in the trunk?","What are the 3 things a sympathetic axon can do in the trunk?","Synapse at the same level; Ascend and synapse at a higher level; Descend and synapse at a lower level"],["","Where is the sympathetic trunk?","Where is the sympathetic trunk?","Bilateral to the spinal cord/vertebral column"]]},{"id":2027,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","f":"nt14","s":"A sympathetic fibre leaves the spinal cord, passes through the sympathetic trunk without synapsing, and runs in a splanchnic nerve. Where does it synapse, and what does it supply?","o":["In a prevertebral ganglion; the abdomen","In the trunk at the same level; skeletal muscle","In a ganglion inside the bladder; the pelvis","In the dorsal root ganglion; the skin","In the brainstem; the head"],"e":"One sympathetic route goes through a <b>splanchnic nerve</b> (<b>without synapsing in the trunk</b>) to a <b>prevertebral ganglion</b>, then <b>to the abdomen</b>.","w":["That route returns to the spinal nerve to the MSK system.","Pelvic parasympathetic pattern.","The DRG is sensory.","Not a sympathetic route."],"k":"Splanchnic route: no trunk synapse → prevertebral ganglion → abdomen.","rn":[["","How do sympathetic fibres reach the abdomen?","How do sympathetic fibres reach the abdomen?","Pass through the trunk without synapsing; Splanchnic nerve; Synapse in prevertebral ganglia"]]},{"id":2028,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","f":"nt14","s":"Which of the following is <b>not</b> a route taken by sympathetic fibres?","o":["Via the pelvic splanchnic nerves to the bladder","Back into the spinal nerve to the musculoskeletal system","To the heart, lungs or head","Via a splanchnic nerve to a prevertebral ganglion, then to the abdomen","Through the spinal nerve into the sympathetic trunk"],"e":"Sympathetic fibres pass through the spinal nerve into the trunk, then go back into the spinal nerve to the <b>musculoskeletal system</b>, to the <b>heart, lungs or head</b>, or via a <b>splanchnic nerve</b> to a prevertebral ganglion and the <b>abdomen</b>. <b>Pelvic splanchnic nerves</b> carry <b>sacral parasympathetic</b> fibres.","w":["A sympathetic route.","A sympathetic route.","A sympathetic route.","Part of the sympathetic pathway."],"k":"Pelvic splanchnic = parasympathetic, not sympathetic.","rn":[["","What are the 2 parasympathetic pathways?","What are the 2 parasympathetic pathways?","Brainstem via cranial nerves; Sacral spinal cord via pelvic splanchnic nerves"]]},{"id":2029,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","f":"nt15","s":"Where are parasympathetic ganglia located, and what does this mean for the lengths of the pre- and postganglionic axons?","o":["Close to the organ; long preganglionic, short postganglionic","Close to the organ; short preganglionic, long postganglionic","Near the vertebral column; long preganglionic, short postganglionic","Near the vertebral column; short preganglionic, long postganglionic","In the sympathetic trunk; pre- and postganglionic of equal length"],"e":"Parasympathetic ganglia are <b>close to (or in) the effector organ</b>, so there are <b>long preganglionic</b> and <b>short postganglionic</b> axons. This is the primary anatomical difference from the sympathetic division.","w":["The ganglion is near the organ, so the preganglionic axon is long.","Parasympathetic ganglia are near the organ.","That is the sympathetic arrangement.","The trunk contains sympathetic ganglia."],"k":"Parasympathetic: long pre, short post (ganglion at the organ).","rn":[["","What is the primary anatomical difference of the parasympathetic division?","What is the primary anatomical difference of the parasympathetic division?","Really long preganglionic axons; Really short postganglionic axons"],["","What does the map of the ANS show about the parasympathetic fibres?","What does the map of the ANS show about the parasympathetic fibres?","Really long preganglionic axons; Synapse very close to target organs; Little branching"]]},{"id":2030,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","f":"nt15","s":"Parasympathetic fibres from the brainstem travel in cranial nerves. Which targets do they supply?","o":["Head, heart, lungs and abdomen","Head, heart, lungs and pelvic organs","Pelvic organs only","Head and neck only","Heart, lungs and adrenal medulla"],"e":"Brainstem parasympathetic fibres travel in <b>cranial nerves</b> to supply the <b>head, heart, lungs and abdomen</b>. Sacral parasympathetic fibres supply the <b>pelvic organs only</b>.","w":["Pelvic organs are supplied by sacral parasympathetic fibres.","Pelvic organs are sacral.","They also supply the heart, lungs and abdomen.","The adrenal medulla has a sympathetic supply."],"k":"Brainstem parasympathetic: head, heart, lungs, abdomen.","rn":[["","What does the brainstem parasympathetic pathway supply?","What does the brainstem parasympathetic pathway supply?","Head; Heart; Lungs; Abdomen"]]},{"id":2031,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","s":"Name the nerves carrying preganglionic parasympathetic axons from the sacral cord to the pelvic organs.","e":"Pelvic splanchnic nerves (sacral parasympathetic) supply bladder, rectum and sexual organs; preganglionic axons are long and synapse on or near the organ.","k":"Pelvic splanchnic nerves.","a":["pelvic splanchnic nerves","pelvic splanchnic","pelvic splanchnics","nervi erigentes"],"rn":[["","What are the 2 parasympathetic pathways?","What are the 2 parasympathetic pathways?","Brainstem via cranial nerves; Sacral spinal cord via pelvic splanchnic nerves"]]},{"id":2032,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","f":"nt16","s":"Damage to the sacral parasympathetic outflow would most directly affect which group of functions?","o":["Urination, defecation and sexual function","Salivation, lacrimation and digestion","Heart rate and airway diameter","Pupil size and lacrimation","Sweating and skin vessel tone"],"e":"Sacral parasympathetic fibres supply the pelvic organs: the <b>bladder (urination)</b>, <b>rectum (defecation)</b> and reproductive organs (<b>sexual function</b>).","w":["Head structures are supplied by brainstem (cranial) fibres.","Heart and lungs are supplied by brainstem fibres.","Pupils are supplied by brainstem fibres.","Skin is supplied by sympathetic fibres in spinal nerves."],"k":"Sacral parasympathetic: bladder, rectum, sexual function.","rn":[["","What are the 3 functions of the sacral parasympathetic?","What are the 3 functions of the sacral parasympathetic?","Bladder: urination; Rectum: defecation; Sexual function"]]},{"id":2033,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","s":"Which function is a good example of interplay between the sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions?","o":["Sexual function","Lacrimation","Thermoregulation","Balance","Satiety"],"e":"<b>Sexual function</b> is a nice example of the <b>interplay between the sympathetic and parasympathetic</b> divisions working together.","w":["Not the example.","Not the example.","Not the example.","Not the example."],"k":"Sexual function = sympathetic/parasympathetic interplay.","rn":[["","Which function is a nice example of sympathetic and parasympathetic interplay?","Which function is a nice example of sympathetic and parasympathetic interplay?","Sexual function; Needs an arousal level to sustain it"]]},{"id":2034,"t":"Somatic Nervous System","f":"nt13","s":"How does the somatic pathway to skeletal muscle differ from a typical autonomic pathway?","o":["It uses one motor neuron with no ganglion","It uses two neurons with a ganglion close to the muscle","It uses two neurons with a ganglion in the sympathetic trunk","It uses a single preganglionic axon like the adrenal medulla","It has no neurons; muscle is stimulated by hormones"],"e":"The somatic diagram shows <b>one motor neuron</b> running from the spinal cord directly to <b>skeletal muscle</b>, with <b>no ganglion</b>. The ANS usually uses two neurons.","w":["Parasympathetic pattern.","Sympathetic pattern.","The adrenal exception is sympathetic.","Not correct."],"k":"Somatic: one motor neuron, no ganglion.","rn":[["","How many neurons run from the spinal cord to skeletal muscle?","How many neurons run from the spinal cord to skeletal muscle?","1 motor neuron; No ganglion"],["","Somatic vs autonomic: neurons to the effector","Somatic vs autonomic: neurons to the effector","Somatic: one motor neuron; Autonomic: usually two"]]},{"id":2035,"t":"Somatic Nervous System","s":"What is meant by “efferent”?","o":["Conducting outwards, away from the CNS","Conducting towards the CNS","Conducting between two hemispheres","Conducting within the dorsal root ganglion","Conducting only sensory information"],"e":"<b>Efferent</b> = conducting <b>outwards</b> (away from the CNS). Afferent = towards the CNS. Memory aid: Afferent = Arriving; Efferent = Exiting.","w":["Afferent.","Not the definition.","Not the definition.","Sensory is afferent."],"k":"Efferent = away from CNS; afferent = towards CNS.","rn":[["","Efferent","Efferent","Conducting outwards; Away from the CNS"]]},{"id":2036,"t":"Somatic Nervous System","s":"What do somatic efferent neurons do?","o":["Generate, conduct and coordinate motor orders to skeletal muscle","Generate, conduct and coordinate motor orders to smooth muscle","Carry sensory information from the skin to the dorsal root","Carry motor orders to glands through autonomic ganglia","Release acetylcholine onto postganglionic neurons"],"e":"Somatic efferent neurons are involved in <b>voluntary control</b>, i.e. <b>contraction of skeletal muscles</b>. They <b>generate, conduct and coordinate motor orders</b>.","w":["Smooth muscle is autonomic.","That is somatosensory (afferent).","Glands are autonomic targets.","That is the preganglionic autonomic neuron."],"k":"Somatic efferent: voluntary skeletal muscle; generate, conduct, coordinate motor orders.","rn":[["","What are the 3 things somatic efferent neurons do with motor orders?","What are the 3 things somatic efferent neurons do with motor orders?","Generate; Conduct; Coordinate"]]},{"id":2037,"t":"Somatic Nervous System","s":"The mnemonic “DAVE” refers to which pairing?","o":["Dorsal = afferent; ventral = efferent","Dorsal = efferent; ventral = afferent","Dorsal = autonomic; ventral = somatic","Dorsal = voluntary; ventral = involuntary","Dermatome = area; ventral = effector"],"e":"<b>DAVE</b>: <b>D</b>orsal = <b>A</b>fferent, <b>V</b>entral = <b>E</b>fferent.","w":["Reversed.","Not the mnemonic.","Not the mnemonic.","Not the mnemonic."],"k":"DAVE: Dorsal Afferent, Ventral Efferent.","rn":[["","DAVE","DAVE","Dorsal Afferent, Ventral Efferent"]]},{"id":2038,"t":"Spinal Nerves","s":"Spinal nerves provide motor and sensory supply to the whole body with one exception. What is it?","o":["The head and parts of the neck","The upper limbs","The pelvic organs","The skin and muscles of the back","The lower limbs"],"e":"Spinal nerves provide <b>motor and sensory supply to the whole body, except the head and parts of the neck</b>, plus <b>sympathetic supply to the whole body</b>.","w":["Supplied by cervical spinal nerves.","Supplied by spinal nerves.","Supplied by dorsal rami of spinal nerves.","Supplied by lumbar and sacral nerves."],"k":"Spinal nerves: whole body except head/parts of neck.","rn":[["","What are the 2 things spinal nerves provide?","What are the 2 things spinal nerves provide?","Motor and sensory supply to the whole body, except head and parts of neck; Sympathetic supply to the whole body"],["","Spinal vs cranial nerves: supply","Spinal vs cranial nerves: supply","Spinal: whole body except head and parts of neck; Cranial: head and neck"]]},{"id":2039,"t":"Spinal Nerves","s":"Spinal nerves provide sympathetic supply to which region?","o":["The whole body","The head only","The pelvic organs only","The thorax only","None; sympathetic fibres travel only in cranial nerves"],"e":"Spinal nerves provide <b>sympathetic supply to the whole body</b>. This is why spinal nerves are mixed: motor, sensory and sympathetic fibres.","w":["Not correct.","Not correct.","Not correct.","Parasympathetic fibres travel in cranial nerves."],"k":"Spinal nerves carry sympathetic supply to the whole body.","rn":[["","What are the 2 things spinal nerves provide?","What are the 2 things spinal nerves provide?","Motor and sensory supply to the whole body, except head and parts of neck; Sympathetic supply to the whole body"]]},{"id":2040,"t":"Spinal Nerves","s":"Why are spinal nerves described as “mixed” nerves?","o":["They contain somatic motor, somatosensory and sympathetic fibres","They contain somatic motor, somatosensory and parasympathetic fibres","They contain somatic motor and somatosensory fibres only","They contain fibres from both cranial and spinal roots","They contain both grey matter and white matter"],"e":"Spinal nerves are <b>mixed</b>: they contain <b>motor, somatosensory and autonomic (sympathetic)</b> fibres.","w":["Spinal nerves carry sympathetic, not parasympathetic, fibres (except sacral outflow).","They also carry sympathetic fibres.","They come from the spinal cord.","Mixed refers to fibre types."],"k":"Mixed = motor + sensory + sympathetic.","rn":[["","What fibres do both primary rami carry?","What fibres do both primary rami carry?","Somatic motor; Somatosensory; Sympathetic"],["","What 3 types of fibre does a spinal nerve contain?","What 3 types of fibre does a spinal nerve contain?","Motor; Sensory; Sympathetic; So they are mixed"]]},{"id":2041,"t":"Spinal Nerves","f":"nt19","s":"Which statement about where spinal nerves exit relative to their vertebrae is correct?","o":["C1–C7 exit above their vertebrae; C8 onwards exit below","C1–C8 exit above their vertebrae; T1 onwards exit below","All spinal nerves exit below their vertebrae","All spinal nerves exit above their vertebrae","C1–C7 exit below their vertebrae; C8 onwards exit above"],"e":"<b>Only 7 cervical spinal nerves (C1–C7) exit above their vertebrae</b>. <b>C8 onwards exit below</b>.","w":["Only 7 cervical nerves exit above; C8 exits below.","C1–C7 exit above.","Only C1–C7 exit above.","Reversed."],"k":"C1–C7 above; C8 onwards below.","rn":[["","Which spinal nerves exit below their vertebrae?","Which spinal nerves exit below their vertebrae?","C8 onwards (C8+)"],["","Which cervical spinal nerves exit above their vertebrae?","Which cervical spinal nerves exit above their vertebrae?","C1 to C7; Only 7"]]},{"id":2042,"t":"Spinal Nerves","f":"nt19","s":"Which spinal nerve region supplies mainly the thorax and abdomen?","o":["Thoracic (12 pairs)","Cervical (8 pairs)","Lumbar (5 pairs)","Sacral (5 pairs)","Coccygeal (1 pair)"],"e":"From the organisation slide: <b>cervical (8)</b> upper limb/head/neck; <b>thoracic (12)</b> thorax/abdomen; <b>lumbar (5)</b> pelvis/lower limb; <b>sacral (5)</b> lower limb; <b>coccygeal (1)</b>.","w":["Upper limb/head/neck.","Pelvis/lower limb.","Lower limb.","No main region listed."],"k":"Thoracic nerves: thorax/abdomen.","rn":[["","What do the thoracic spinal nerves supply?","What do the thoracic spinal nerves supply?","Thorax; Abdomen"]]},{"id":2043,"t":"Spinal Nerves","s":"A spinal nerve is labelled “T10”. How does this naming convention differ from that of cranial nerves?","o":["Spinal: region letter plus number; cranial: Roman numerals I–XII","Spinal: Roman numerals; cranial: region letter plus number","Spinal: region letter plus number; cranial: Arabic numbers 1–12","Spinal: named after muscles supplied; cranial: Roman numerals","Spinal: Roman numerals I–XXXI; cranial: Roman numerals I–XII"],"e":"Spinal nerves are named by <b>region letter + number</b> (e.g. <b>T10</b>), whereas cranial nerves are numbered <b>I–XII in Roman numerals</b>.","w":["Reversed.","Cranial nerves use Roman numerals.","Spinal nerves use letter + number.","Spinal nerves use letter + number."],"k":"Spinal: letter + number; cranial: Roman numerals.","rn":[["","Spinal vs cranial nerves: naming","Spinal vs cranial nerves: naming","Spinal: region letter + number; Cranial: Roman numerals I to XII"]]},{"id":2044,"t":"Spinal Nerves","s":"What is meant by “excellent segmental organisation” of the spinal nerves?","o":["One bilateral pair of spinal nerves per vertebra","There is one spinal nerve to each internal organ","There is one dermatome to each vertebral region","Each spinal nerve contains only one type of fibre","Each segment of the cord has its own separate ganglion chain"],"e":"Spinal nerves show <b>excellent segmental organisation</b>, with <b>one pair (bilateral) to each vertebra</b>. This segmental arrangement produces dermatomes and myotomes.","w":["Spinal nerves supply regions, not one organ each.","Dermatomes result from segmental organisation; it is not the definition.","Spinal nerves are mixed.","The sympathetic trunk is one chain."],"k":"Segmental organisation: one pair per vertebra.","rn":[["","How are spinal nerves organised?","How are spinal nerves organised?","Excellent segmental organisation; One pair (bilateral) to each vertebra"]]},{"id":2045,"t":"Spinal Nerves","s":"Which statement correctly contrasts spinal and cranial nerves?","o":["Spinal: 31 pairs from the spinal cord; cranial: 12 pairs from brain/brainstem","Spinal: 12 pairs from the brainstem; cranial: 31 pairs from the spinal cord","Spinal: 31 pairs from the spinal cord; cranial: 12 pairs from the cerebellum","Spinal: 33 pairs from the spinal cord; cranial: 12 pairs from brain/brainstem","Spinal: 31 pairs from the spinal cord; cranial: 10 pairs from brain/brainstem"],"e":"<b>Spinal nerves</b>: <b>31 pairs</b>, from the <b>spinal cord</b>, named by letter + number, supply the whole body except head/parts of the neck. <b>Cranial nerves</b>: <b>12 pairs</b>, from the <b>brain/brainstem</b>, Roman numerals, supply the head and neck.","w":["Reversed.","Cranial nerves arise from the brain/brainstem.","There are 31 pairs.","There are 12 pairs."],"k":"Spinal 31 (cord); cranial 12 (brain/brainstem).","rn":[["","Spinal vs cranial nerves: origin","Spinal vs cranial nerves: origin","Spinal: spinal cord; Cranial: brain/brainstem"],["","Spinal vs cranial nerves: number","Spinal vs cranial nerves: number","Spinal: 31 pairs; Cranial: 12 pairs"]]},{"id":2046,"t":"Spinal Nerves","f":"nt20","s":"Why does a “horse's tail” of nerve roots form inside the lower vertebral canal?","o":["The cord ends around L1/L2, so lower roots run down the canal","The cord ends at the sacrum, so roots fan out like a tail","The sympathetic trunk ends in the sacrum and forms the roots","The dorsal root ganglia of lower levels lie inside the canal","The cord ends around T12, so cranial nerves descend instead"],"e":"The <b>cauda equina</b> forms because the <b>spinal cord ends around L1/L2</b>, so nerve roots for the lower levels have to run down inside the vertebral canal before exiting.","w":["The cord ends around L1/L2.","The cauda equina is spinal nerve roots.","The cauda equina is roots running to their exits.","Cranial nerves are not involved."],"k":"Cauda equina: cord ends ~L1/L2, roots continue down.","rn":[["","Why does the cauda equina form?","Why does the cauda equina form?","Spinal cord ends around L1/L2; Lower nerve roots run down the vertebral canal"]]},{"id":2047,"t":"Spinal Nerves","s":"Which nerve roots make up the cauda equina?","o":["L2–L5, S1–S5 and the coccygeal nerve","L1–L5 and S1–S5 only","T12–L5 and S1–S5","S1–S5 and the coccygeal nerve only","L2–L5 and S1–S5 only"],"e":"The cauda equina is a bundle of spinal nerves and their roots from <b>L2–L5, S1–S5</b> and the <b>coccygeal nerve</b>.","w":["It starts at L2 and includes the coccygeal nerve.","It starts at L2.","It also includes L2–L5.","It includes the coccygeal nerve."],"k":"Cauda equina: L2–L5, S1–S5, Co.","rn":[["","Cauda Equina","Cauda Equina","Latin for horse's tail; Bundle of spinal nerves and their roots; L2 to L5, S1 to S5 and the coccygeal nerve"]]},{"id":2048,"t":"Spinal Nerves","s":"A patient has compression of the structure labelled <b>A</b>. Which group of structures is most likely affected?","o":["Pelvis, lower limbs and the anal sphincters","Pelvis, upper limbs and the anal sphincters","Lower limbs and the diaphragm","Thorax, abdomen and lower limbs","Head, neck and upper limbs"],"e":"The cauda equina is responsible for innervation of the <b>pelvis and lower limbs</b>, and the <b>internal/external anal sphincters</b>.","w":["Upper limbs are cervical.","The diaphragm is C3–C5.","Thorax/abdomen is thoracic.","Head/neck/upper limb is cervical/cranial."],"k":"Cauda equina: pelvis, lower limbs, anal sphincters.","img":{"src":"2/cauda","cap":"Lower vertebral canal opened from behind (dissection). Labels removed."},"rn":[["","What are the 3 things the cauda equina innervates?","What are the 3 things the cauda equina innervates?","Pelvis; Lower limbs; Internal/external anal sphincters"]]},{"id":2049,"t":"Spinal Nerves","s":"What does “cauda equina” mean?","o":["Horse's tail","Tree of life","Little brain","Opening or passage","Star cell"],"e":"<i>Cauda equina</i> = Latin for “<b>horse's tail</b>”, because of its appearance.","w":["Arbor vitae.","Cerebellum.","Foramen.","Astrocyte."],"k":"Cauda equina = horse's tail.","rn":[["","Cauda Equina","Cauda Equina","Latin for horse's tail; Bundle of spinal nerves and their roots; L2 to L5, S1 to S5 and the coccygeal nerve"]]},{"id":2050,"t":"Somatic Nervous System","s":"Name the root labelled <b>A</b>, which carries sensory (afferent) fibres into the cord.","e":"A is the dorsal root: afferent (sensory). The ventral root (C) is efferent (motor and sympathetic). Mnemonic DAVE: dorsal afferent, ventral efferent.","k":"A = dorsal root.","img":{"src":"2/roots","cap":"Transverse section of the spinal cord with its roots and nerves. Labels removed."},"a":["dorsal root","posterior root","dorsal","sensory root"],"rn":[["","DAVE","DAVE","Dorsal Afferent, Ventral Efferent"],["","Dorsal Root","Dorsal Root","Afferent; Somatosensory; Incoming information"],["","Dorsal Root Ganglion","Dorsal Root Ganglion","A cluster of cell bodies; On the dorsal root"]]},{"id":2051,"t":"Spinal Nerves","s":"A lesion destroys root <b>C</b> at one level but spares the root on the opposite surface. What type of fibres are lost?","o":["Efferent: somatic motor and sympathetic","Afferent: somatosensory only","Efferent: somatic motor only","Efferent: parasympathetic only","Afferent and efferent: all fibre types"],"e":"The <b>ventral root</b> is <b>efferent</b>, carrying <b>somatic motor and sympathetic</b> axons away from the CNS. The dorsal root is afferent (somatosensory).","w":["Afferent fibres are in the dorsal root.","The ventral root also carries sympathetic fibres.","Spinal sympathetic fibres leave in the ventral root.","The dorsal root is spared."],"k":"Ventral root: efferent (somatic motor + sympathetic).","img":{"src":"2/roots","cap":"Transverse section of the spinal cord with its roots and nerves. Labels removed."},"rn":[["","Ventral Root","Ventral Root","Efferent; Somatic motor/sympathetic; Messages away from the CNS"]]},{"id":2052,"t":"Spinal Nerves","s":"Sensory information from the skin enters the spinal cord. Through which root does it pass, and where are the sensory cell bodies?","o":["Dorsal root; dorsal root ganglion","Ventral root; ventral horn","Dorsal root; sympathetic trunk","Ventral root; dorsal root ganglion","Ventral ramus; prevertebral ganglion"],"e":"Sensory information enters via the <b>dorsal root</b> (afferent, somatosensory). The <b>dorsal root ganglion</b> (cluster of cell bodies) sits on the dorsal root.","w":["The ventral root is efferent.","The trunk is sympathetic.","The ventral root is efferent.","Sympathetic."],"k":"Sensory in via dorsal root; cell bodies in DRG.","rn":[["","Dorsal Root Ganglion","Dorsal Root Ganglion","A cluster of cell bodies; On the dorsal root"]]},{"id":2053,"t":"Spinal Nerves","s":"Which sequence correctly describes the formation of a spinal nerve and its branches?","o":["Rootlets → roots → spinal nerve → rami","Roots → rootlets → spinal nerve → rami","Rootlets → roots → rami → spinal nerve","Rootlets → spinal nerve → roots → rami","Rami → spinal nerve → roots → rootlets"],"e":"The smallest parts are the <b>rootlets</b>, which join to form the <b>roots</b>, which form the <b>spinal nerve</b> (the trunk), which divides into <b>branches</b> (the <b>rami</b>).","w":["Rootlets are smallest.","The trunk (spinal nerve) comes before branches (rami).","Roots form the spinal nerve.","This is reversed."],"k":"Rootlets → roots → spinal nerve → rami.","rn":[["","What is the full route from spinal cord to primary rami?","What is the full route from spinal cord to primary rami?","Nerve rootlets; Nerve roots (with DRG); Spinal nerve; Dorsal and ventral primary rami"],["","If someone has pain at T5, how do you trace it back?","If someone has pain at T5, how do you trace it back?","Receptor; Primary rami; Spinal nerve; Nerve root/rootlets for that area"],["","What is the route of sympathetic fibres from the spinal cord?","What is the route of sympathetic fibres from the spinal cord?","Nerve rootlets; Nerve root; Spinal nerve; Sympathetic trunk"]]},{"id":2054,"t":"Spinal Nerves","s":"Name the opening through which a spinal nerve leaves the vertebral column.","e":"Spinal nerves leave through the intervertebral foramen (foramen = an opening or passage).","k":"Intervertebral foramen.","a":["intervertebral foramen","intervertebral foramina","neural foramen"],"rn":[["","How do spinal nerves exit the vertebral column?","How do spinal nerves exit the vertebral column?","Through the intervertebral foramen"]]},{"id":2055,"t":"Spinal Nerves","s":"Which statement about the length of a spinal nerve is correct?","o":["A spinal nerve is very short before dividing into rami","A spinal nerve runs the full length of the limb","A spinal nerve extends from C1 to L1","A spinal nerve is longer than the sympathetic trunk","A spinal nerve has no fixed length because it is part of the CNS"],"e":"The slide states that a spinal nerve is <b>very short</b>; it divides into dorsal and ventral primary rami.","w":["Branches continue; the nerve itself is short.","That is the spinal cord.","Not correct.","Spinal nerves are PNS."],"k":"Spinal nerve is very short.","rn":[["","How long is a spinal nerve?","How long is a spinal nerve?","Very short"],["","What is the full route from spinal cord to primary rami?","What is the full route from spinal cord to primary rami?","Nerve rootlets; Nerve roots (with DRG); Spinal nerve; Dorsal and ventral primary rami"]]},{"id":2056,"t":"Spinal Nerves","s":"Name the branch of a spinal nerve that supplies the skin over the paravertebral gutter, erector spinae and the facet joints.","e":"The dorsal primary ramus is small and supplies the back; the ventral primary ramus is larger, supplying the front and sides of the trunk and the limbs.","k":"Dorsal primary ramus.","a":["dorsal primary ramus","dorsal ramus","posterior primary ramus","posterior ramus"],"rn":[["","What are the 3 things the dorsal primary ramus innervates?","What are the 3 things the dorsal primary ramus innervates?","Skin over paravertebral gutter; Erector spinae muscles; Facet joints of vertebral column"],["","Dorsal primary ramus","Dorsal primary ramus","Posterior primary ramus"],["","Which ramus supplies the strip down the back?","Which ramus supplies the strip down the back?","Dorsal primary ramus"]]},{"id":2057,"t":"Spinal Nerves","f":"nt26","s":"Why is the ventral primary ramus much larger than the dorsal primary ramus?","o":["It supplies the rest of the body except the head and parts of the neck","It supplies the head, neck and the rest of the body","It carries all the sympathetic fibres; the dorsal ramus carries none","It contains the dorsal root ganglion within it","It supplies the skin of the back and the erector spinae"],"e":"The ventral primary ramus is <b>much larger</b> because of the role it undertakes: it supplies <b>the rest of the body</b> (skin and musculoskeletal system), except the head/parts of the neck.","w":["Not the head/parts of neck.","Both rami carry sympathetic fibres.","The DRG is on the dorsal root.","That is the dorsal primary ramus."],"k":"Ventral ramus: much larger, supplies the rest of the body.","rn":[["","What does the ventral primary ramus supply?","What does the ventral primary ramus supply?","Rest of the body; Except head/parts of neck; Skin and musculoskeletal system"]]},{"id":2058,"t":"Spinal Nerves","s":"Which types of fibre are carried in <b>both</b> the dorsal and ventral primary rami?","o":["Somatic motor, somatosensory and sympathetic","Somatic motor, somatosensory and parasympathetic","Somatic motor and somatosensory only","Somatosensory and sympathetic only","Somatic motor and sympathetic only"],"e":"Both rami carry <b>somatic motor, somatosensory and sympathetic</b> fibres.","w":["Spinal nerve rami carry sympathetic fibres.","They also carry sympathetic fibres.","They also carry somatic motor fibres.","They also carry somatosensory fibres."],"k":"Both rami: motor + sensory + sympathetic.","rn":[["","What fibres do both primary rami carry?","What fibres do both primary rami carry?","Somatic motor; Somatosensory; Sympathetic"]]},{"id":2059,"t":"Spinal Nerves","s":"A patient has a problem affecting only the dorsal primary ramus at one level. Which of the following would be affected?","o":["The facet joints at that level","The skin of the anterior abdominal wall","The intercostal muscles","The muscles of the upper limb","The pelvic organs"],"e":"The dorsal ramus supplies the <b>skin over the paravertebral gutter</b>, <b>erector spinae</b> and <b>facet joints</b>. The others are supplied by ventral rami (or pelvic splanchnic nerves).","w":["Ventral rami.","Intercostal nerves (ventral rami).","Ventral rami.","Sacral parasympathetic."],"k":"Dorsal ramus territory = back only.","rn":[["","What are the 3 things the dorsal primary ramus innervates?","What are the 3 things the dorsal primary ramus innervates?","Skin over paravertebral gutter; Erector spinae muscles; Facet joints of vertebral column"]]},{"id":2060,"t":"Spinal Nerves","s":"What is a “foramen”?","o":["An opening or passage","A cluster of cell bodies","A large groove on the brain","A branch of a spinal nerve","A group of muscles"],"e":"<b>Foramen</b> = an opening or passage, e.g. the <b>intervertebral foramen</b>.","w":["Ganglion.","Fissure.","Ramus.","Myotome."],"k":"Foramen = opening/passage.","rn":[["","Foramen","Foramen","Opening or passage; Gap or hole things pass through"]]},{"id":2061,"t":"Dermatomes and Myotome​","s":"Name the area of skin supplied by a single spinal nerve.","e":"A dermatome is an area of skin supplied by one spinal nerve (input to the dorsal root); there is overlap and variation between people.","k":"Dermatome.","a":["dermatome","dermatomes"],"rn":[["","Dermatome vs myotome","Dermatome vs myotome","Dermatome: area of skin; Myotome: set of muscles; Both: single spinal nerve"],["","Dermatome","Dermatome","Area of skin innervated by a single spinal nerve (primary ramus); Sensory input to the dorsal root"]]},{"id":2062,"t":"Dermatomes and Myotome​","s":"A patient has a band of numbness at the level of the umbilicus. Name the spinal nerve most likely involved.","e":"The T10 dermatome is at the umbilicus.","k":"T10.","a":["t10","t 10","tenth thoracic","10th thoracic"],"rn":[["","T10","T10","Umbilicus"]]},{"id":2063,"t":"Dermatomes and Myotome​","s":"Loss of sensation in a specific dermatome points to injury at which possible sites?","o":["The spinal cord, the nerve root or the spinal nerve","The spinal cord, the cerebellum or the brainstem","The sympathetic trunk or the adrenal medulla","The cranial nerves or the brainstem","The ventral root only"],"e":"<b>Loss or altered sensation and/or pain</b> from a dermatome indicates possible injury to the <b>spinal cord</b>, <b>nerve root</b> or <b>spinal nerve</b>. Knowing dermatomes helps locate the injury.","w":["The cerebellum and brainstem were not listed.","These are autonomic structures.","Dermatomes are spinal.","The dorsal root is sensory; any of cord, root or nerve."],"k":"Dermatomal loss → cord, root or spinal nerve injury.","rn":[["","What does loss/altered sensation or pain from a dermatome indicate?","What does loss/altered sensation or pain from a dermatome indicate?","Possible injury to the spinal cord; Nerve root (or rootlets); Or spinal nerve"]]},{"id":2064,"t":"Dermatomes and Myotome​","s":"A clinician uses a dermatome map to locate a spinal nerve injury but finds the patient's sensory loss does not match the textbook map exactly. Which explanation applies?","o":["There is significant overlap and variation between people","Dermatome maps apply only to children","Dermatomes change with sympathetic activity","Each dermatome is supplied by both dorsal and ventral roots","Dermatomes are supplied by cranial nerves in adults"],"e":"Dermatome maps are <b>“really good on paper”</b>, but in practice there is a <b>significant amount of overlap and variation</b> in the population.","w":["Not stated.","Not stated.","Sensory input is to the dorsal root.","Dermatomes are spinal nerve territories."],"k":"Dermatomes: overlap and variation.","rn":[["","What is the caveat about dermatome maps?","What is the caveat about dermatome maps?","Significant overlap; Variation"]]},{"id":2065,"t":"Dermatomes and Myotome​","s":"Name the nerves (ventral primary rami of T1–T11) that supply the dermatomes on the front and sides of the trunk.","e":"Intercostal nerves are the ventral primary rami of T1–T11; they supply the intercostal muscles (a simple segmental myotome) and T1–T11 dermatomes.","k":"Intercostal nerves.","a":["intercostal nerves","intercostal","intercostal nerve"],"rn":[["","Intercostal Nerves","Intercostal Nerves","Ventral primary rami of T1 to T11; Innervate T1 to T11 dermatomes"],["","What is the example of a myotome?","What is the example of a myotome?","Intercostal muscles; Supplied by intercostal nerves (T1 to T11)"]]},{"id":2066,"t":"Dermatomes and Myotome​","s":"A strip of skin over the back, next to the spine, is supplied by which branch?","o":["Dorsal primary rami","Ventral primary rami","Intercostal nerves","Pelvic splanchnic nerves","Cranial nerves"],"e":"The back strip on the dermatome map is supplied by the <b>dorsal primary rami</b> (skin over the paravertebral gutter).","w":["Rest of the body.","Ventral rami T1–T11.","Pelvic organs.","Head and neck."],"k":"Back skin strip = dorsal rami.","rn":[["","Which ramus supplies the strip down the back?","Which ramus supplies the strip down the back?","Dorsal primary ramus"],["","What is the full route from spinal cord to primary rami?","What is the full route from spinal cord to primary rami?","Nerve rootlets; Nerve roots (with DRG); Spinal nerve; Dorsal and ventral primary rami"],["","How do you remember the 2 primary rami?","How do you remember the 2 primary rami?","Dorsal = Down your back only; Ventral = Very big; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":2067,"t":"Dermatomes and Myotome​","s":"Why do dermatomes and myotomes exist?","o":["Because of the segmental organisation of the spinal nerves","Because spinal and cranial nerves overlap in the trunk","Because the sympathetic trunk has a ganglion at every level","Because skin and muscle are both supplied by the cauda equina","Because each vertebra has its own dorsal root ganglion"],"e":"The <b>segmental organisation</b> of spinal nerves results in <b>dermatomes</b> (skin) and <b>myotomes</b> (muscle).","w":["Cranial overlap was not the explanation.","The trunk is not the reason.","The cauda equina is only lower roots.","DRGs exist but the reason is segmental organisation."],"k":"Segmental organisation → dermatomes and myotomes.","rn":[["","What does the segmental organisation of spinal nerves result in?","What does the segmental organisation of spinal nerves result in?","Dermatomes; Myotomes"]]},{"id":2068,"t":"Dermatomes and Myotome​","f":"nt29","s":"A patient with irritation of the diaphragm complains of pain at the tip of the shoulder. Which dermatomes explain this?","o":["C3–C5","T10","T1–T11","L1–L2","S1–S5"],"e":"<b>Visceral pain</b> can be <b>referred to the skin</b> as cutaneous pain in a dermatome: <b>diaphragmatic irritation</b> is referred to the <b>shoulder</b> (<b>C3–C5 dermatomes</b>).","w":["Umbilicus.","Intercostal dermatomes.","Not the shoulder.","Sacral."],"k":"Diaphragm irritation → shoulder pain (C3–C5).","rn":[["","What is the example of referred pain?","What is the example of referred pain?","Diaphragmatic irritation; Referred to shoulder; C3 to C5 dermatomes"]]},{"id":2069,"t":"Dermatomes and Myotome​","s":"Which statement best describes referred pain?","o":["Visceral pain felt as cutaneous pain in a dermatome","Cutaneous pain felt as visceral pain in an organ","Visceral pain felt only over the damaged organ","Cutaneous pain spreading to neighbouring dermatomes","Visceral pain carried to the brainstem by the sympathetic trunk"],"e":"Referred pain: <b>visceral (organ) pain</b> can be <b>referred to the skin</b> (dermatomes) as <b>cutaneous pain</b>.","w":["Reversed.","That would not be referred.","Referred pain is from organ to skin.","Not the definition given."],"k":"Referred pain: visceral → cutaneous (dermatome).","rn":[["","Referred Pain","Referred Pain","Visceral (organ) pain referred to the skin (dermatomes); Felt as cutaneous pain"]]},{"id":2070,"t":"Dermatomes and Myotome​","s":"In simple terms, why might the brain “blame” a patch of skin for pain coming from an organ?","o":["Organ pain arrives at the same spinal levels as that skin","Organ pain travels in the same cranial nerve as that skin","The organ is joined to the skin by an autonomic ganglion","The organ has no sensory supply of its own","The skin and organ share the same myotome"],"e":"Organ pain arrives at the <b>same spinal levels</b> as signals from a patch of skin, so the brain “blames” the skin. Irritate the diaphragm and you feel it in the shoulder because both share C3–C5.","w":["They share spinal levels (e.g. C3–C5).","No such connection.","The organ pain is real and arrives at spinal levels.","Myotomes are muscles."],"k":"Shared spinal levels explain referred pain.","rn":[["","Referred Pain","Referred Pain","Visceral (organ) pain referred to the skin (dermatomes); Felt as cutaneous pain"]]},{"id":2071,"t":"Dermatomes and Myotome​","s":"Name the set of muscles supplied by a single spinal nerve.","e":"A myotome is a set of muscles supplied by one spinal nerve; simple and segmental in the trunk, complex in the limbs.","k":"Myotome.","a":["myotome","myotomes"],"rn":[["","Dermatome vs myotome","Dermatome vs myotome","Dermatome: area of skin; Myotome: set of muscles; Both: single spinal nerve"],["","Myotome","Myotome","A set of muscles innervated by a specific, single spinal nerve"]]},{"id":2072,"t":"Dermatomes and Myotome​","s":"Why are myotomes more complicated in the limbs than in the trunk?","o":["Several spinal nerves supply large limb muscle groups","Limb muscles are supplied by cranial nerves","Limb muscles are supplied only by the dorsal rami","Limb muscles are smooth muscle rather than skeletal muscle","Each limb muscle has its own sympathetic chain"],"e":"Myotomes are <b>more complicated in limbs</b>: <b>more than one spinal nerve</b> innervates large muscle groups.","w":["Limbs are supplied by spinal nerves.","The ventral rami supply the limbs.","Limb muscles are skeletal.","Not stated."],"k":"Limb muscles: multiple spinal nerves.","rn":[["","Why are myotomes more complicated in limbs?","Why are myotomes more complicated in limbs?","More than 1 spinal nerve innervates large muscle groups"]]},{"id":2073,"t":"Dermatomes and Myotome​","f":"nt30","s":"Which is an example of a simple, segmental myotome?","o":["The intercostal muscles, supplied by intercostal nerves (T1–T11)","The diaphragm, supplied by intercostal nerves (T1–T11)","The erector spinae, supplied by intercostal nerves (T1–T11)","The intercostal muscles, supplied by dorsal rami (T1–T11)","The limb muscles, supplied by ventral rami (C5–T1)"],"e":"The <b>intercostal muscles</b> are supplied by the <b>intercostal nerves (T1–T11)</b>; each muscle group is innervated by an individual spinal nerve.","w":["The diaphragm is C3–C5.","Erector spinae are dorsal ramus territory.","Intercostal nerves are ventral rami.","Limbs were the complicated example."],"k":"Intercostal muscles: T1–T11 myotomes.","rn":[["","What is the example of a myotome?","What is the example of a myotome?","Intercostal muscles; Supplied by intercostal nerves (T1 to T11)"]]},{"id":2074,"t":"Dermatomes and Myotome​","s":"Besides its meaning in nerve supply, the term “myotome” is also used in which other context?","o":["Embryology: the part of the somite that develops into muscle","Embryology: the part of the somite that develops into skin","Histology: the layers of myelin around a motor axon","Neuroanatomy: the cortex that controls voluntary movement","Embryology: the part of the neural tube that forms motor neurons"],"e":"The term is also used in <b>embryology</b> for the part of the <b>somite</b> that develops into muscles.","w":["That is not the myotome.","Myelin is not a myotome.","Not the second meaning given.","The somite is the structure."],"k":"Myotome also = part of somite forming muscle.","rn":[["","What does myotome mean in embryology?","What does myotome mean in embryology?","Part of the somite that develops into the muscles"]]},{"id":2080,"t":"Spinal Nerves","s":"Name the bundle of nerve roots labelled <b>A</b>, filling the lower vertebral canal below the end of the cord.","e":"A is the cauda equina (“horse's tail”): lumbar, sacral and coccygeal nerve roots that descend below the end of the cord (L1–L2) to reach their foramina, because the vertebral column grows longer than the cord.","k":"A = cauda equina.","img":{"src":"2/cauda","cap":"Lower vertebral canal opened from behind (dissection). Labels removed."},"a":["cauda equina"],"rn":[["","Why does the cauda equina form?","Why does the cauda equina form?","Spinal cord ends around L1/L2; Lower nerve roots run down the vertebral canal"],["","Cauda Equina","Cauda Equina","Latin for horse's tail; Bundle of spinal nerves and their roots; L2 to L5, S1 to S5 and the coccygeal nerve"],["","How does the cauda equina differ from the spinal cord?","How does the cauda equina differ from the spinal cord?","Not as compacted"]]},{"id":2081,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","s":"A patient's postganglionic parasympathetic axon to the heart is examined. Compared with a sympathetic postganglionic axon to the same organ, what would you expect?","o":["Shorter, because its ganglion lies close to or in the organ","Longer, because its ganglion lies close to or in the organ","Shorter, because its ganglion lies in the sympathetic trunk","Longer, because its ganglion lies in the sympathetic trunk","Absent, because the heart has a single-axon supply"],"e":"Parasympathetic ganglia lie <b>close to or in the effector organ</b>, so parasympathetic <b>postganglionic axons are short</b> (and preganglionic axons long). Sympathetic ganglia lie near the vertebral column.","w":["A ganglion near the organ makes the postganglionic axon short.","Parasympathetic ganglia are not in the trunk.","Parasympathetic ganglia are not in the trunk.","Only the adrenal medulla has a single-axon supply."],"k":"Parasympathetic postganglionic axons are short.","rn":[["","Postganglionic Neuron","Postganglionic Neuron","From the ganglion to the effector organ"],["","What does the map of the ANS show about the parasympathetic fibres?","What does the map of the ANS show about the parasympathetic fibres?","Really long preganglionic axons; Synapse very close to target organs; Little branching"],["","Where are parasympathetic ganglia located?","Where are parasympathetic ganglia located?","Close to the effector organ; Sometimes in the wall of the organ"]]},{"id":2082,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","s":"A student argues that the heart receives parasympathetic supply from the sacral cord. What is the correct source?","o":["The brainstem, via cranial nerves","The thoracic spinal cord","The lumbar spinal cord","The sacral cord via pelvic splanchnic nerves","The adrenal medulla"],"e":"Parasympathetic fibres from the <b>brainstem</b> travel in <b>cranial nerves</b> to the head, <b>heart</b>, lungs and abdomen. Sacral parasympathetic fibres supply the pelvic organs only.","w":["Sympathetic origin.","Sympathetic origin.","Pelvic organs only.","Not a nerve supply."],"k":"Heart parasympathetic: brainstem via cranial nerves.","rn":[["","What are the 2 parasympathetic pathways?","What are the 2 parasympathetic pathways?","Brainstem via cranial nerves; Sacral spinal cord via pelvic splanchnic nerves"]]},{"id":2083,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","s":"Which pairing of ANS division with ganglion location is correct?","o":["Sympathetic – paravertebral or prevertebral ganglia","Parasympathetic – paravertebral ganglia in the trunk","Sympathetic – ganglia inside the target organ","Parasympathetic – prevertebral ganglia only","Somatic – paravertebral ganglia"],"e":"Sympathetic ganglia are in the <b>sympathetic trunk (paravertebral)</b> or are <b>prevertebral</b> ganglia. Parasympathetic ganglia are close to or in the target organ. Somatic pathways have no ganglion.","w":["Parasympathetic ganglia are near organs.","That is parasympathetic.","Parasympathetic ganglia are near/in organs.","Somatic has no ganglion."],"k":"Sympathetic: paravertebral/prevertebral ganglia.","rn":[["","Paravertebral vs prevertebral ganglia","Paravertebral vs prevertebral ganglia","Paravertebral: form the sympathetic trunk; Prevertebral: reached by splanchnic nerves"]]},{"id":2084,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","s":"A drug blocks transmission at all autonomic ganglia. Which structure would still receive sympathetic stimulation through its usual nerve pathway?","o":["The adrenal medulla","The heart","The pupils","The airways","Blood vessels to skeletal muscle"],"e":"The <b>adrenal medulla</b> is supplied by a <b>single preganglionic axon</b> that synapses directly on cells within the medulla, with <b>no ganglionic synapse</b>. All the others depend on a synapse in an autonomic ganglion.","w":["Two-neuron chain via a ganglion.","Two-neuron chain.","Two-neuron chain.","Two-neuron chain."],"k":"Adrenal medulla bypasses the ganglion.","rn":[["","How is the adrenal medulla supplied?","How is the adrenal medulla supplied?","A single preganglionic axon only; Monosynaptic pathway"],["","Which cells does the preganglionic axon innervate in the adrenal medulla?","Which cells does the preganglionic axon innervate in the adrenal medulla?","Chromaffin cells"]]},{"id":2085,"t":"Somatic Nervous System","s":"Which statement about the somatic and autonomic systems is correct?","o":["Somatic: voluntary, one neuron; autonomic: involuntary, usually two","Somatic: voluntary, two neurons; autonomic: involuntary, usually one","Somatic: involuntary, one neuron; autonomic: voluntary, usually two","Somatic: voluntary, one neuron; autonomic: voluntary, usually two","Somatic: involuntary, two neurons; autonomic: involuntary, one"],"e":"<b>Somatic</b>: voluntary, skeletal muscle, one motor neuron. <b>Autonomic</b>: involuntary, smooth muscle, cardiac muscle and glands, usually two neurons (pre- and postganglionic).","w":["Neuron numbers reversed.","Voluntary/involuntary reversed.","The ANS is involuntary.","Somatic is voluntary with one neuron."],"k":"Somatic vs autonomic: control, effector, number of neurons.","rn":[["","Somatic vs autonomic: control","Somatic vs autonomic: control","Somatic: voluntary; Autonomic: involuntary"],["","Somatic vs autonomic: neurons to the effector","Somatic vs autonomic: neurons to the effector","Somatic: one motor neuron; Autonomic: usually two"]]},{"id":2086,"t":"Autonomic Nervous System","s":"A person in a resting state would show which combination?","o":["Increased salivation, increased digestion, decreased heart rate","Increased salivation, decreased digestion, decreased heart rate","Decreased salivation, increased digestion, increased heart rate","Increased salivation, increased digestion, increased heart rate","Decreased salivation, decreased digestion, increased heart rate"],"e":"At rest the <b>parasympathetic</b> (rest and digest) division dominates: <b>salivation</b>, <b>digestion</b> and <b>decreased HR</b>, among others.","w":["Digestion increases at rest.","Salivation increases and HR decreases at rest.","HR decreases at rest.","This is the sympathetic pattern."],"k":"Rest and digest: salivation, digestion, ↓HR.","rn":[["","What are the 6 parasympathetic effects?","What are the 6 parasympathetic effects?","Salivation; Lacrimation; Urination; Digestion; Defecation; Decreased HR, airway diameter, pupil diameter; MNEMONIC"],["","Compare sympathetic vs parasympathetic effect on the heart","Compare sympathetic vs parasympathetic effect on the heart","Sympathetic: increases cardiac functioning; Parasympathetic: decreases HR"],["","HR","HR","Heart Rate"]]},{"id":2087,"t":"Spinal Nerves","s":"Which statement about the spinal cord and the cauda equina is correct?","o":["The cord ends around L1/L2; roots of L2–Co form the cauda equina","The cord ends around T12; roots of L1–Co form the cauda equina","The cord ends around S2; roots of S3–Co form the cauda equina","The cord ends around L1/L2; the cauda equina is part of the CNS","The cord ends around L5; roots of S1–S5 form the cauda equina"],"e":"The <b>spinal cord ends around L1/L2</b>, so the roots for lower levels (<b>L2–L5, S1–S5, Co</b>) run down inside the vertebral canal as the <b>cauda equina</b>.","w":["The cord ends around L1/L2.","The cord ends around L1/L2.","It is a bundle of spinal nerves and roots (PNS).","The cord ends around L1/L2."],"k":"Cord ends L1/L2; cauda equina below.","rn":[["","Why does the cauda equina form?","Why does the cauda equina form?","Spinal cord ends around L1/L2; Lower nerve roots run down the vertebral canal"]]},{"id":2088,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","s":"Which pairing of nerve with its fibre type is correct?","o":["Pelvic splanchnic nerves – parasympathetic","Splanchnic nerves to prevertebral ganglia – parasympathetic","Intercostal nerves – sacral parasympathetic","Dorsal root – efferent motor","Ventral root – somatosensory"],"e":"<b>Pelvic splanchnic nerves</b> carry <b>sacral parasympathetic</b> preganglionic fibres. Splanchnic nerves to prevertebral ganglia are <b>sympathetic</b>; intercostal nerves are ventral rami T1–T11; the dorsal root is afferent; the ventral root is efferent.","w":["Sympathetic.","Ventral rami of T1–T11.","The dorsal root is afferent.","The ventral root is efferent."],"k":"Pelvic splanchnic = parasympathetic; (other) splanchnic = sympathetic.","rn":[["","What are the 2 parasympathetic pathways?","What are the 2 parasympathetic pathways?","Brainstem via cranial nerves; Sacral spinal cord via pelvic splanchnic nerves"]]},{"id":2089,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","s":"A patient's sweating and blood vessel tone in the skin of the leg depend on sympathetic fibres. By which route do these fibres reach the leg?","o":["From the sympathetic trunk back into the spinal nerve","Through the pelvic splanchnic nerves to the leg","Through the cranial nerves from the brainstem","From the adrenal medulla through the blood","Through the dorsal root ganglion to the skin"],"e":"One sympathetic route goes from the trunk <b>back into the spinal nerve to the musculoskeletal system</b>. Spinal nerves provide <b>sympathetic supply to the whole body</b>.","w":["Pelvic splanchnics are parasympathetic.","Cranial nerves supply head structures.","The question asks for a fibre route.","The DRG holds sensory cell bodies."],"k":"Sympathetic to limbs: trunk → back into spinal nerve.","rn":[["","How do sympathetic fibres reach the musculoskeletal system?","How do sympathetic fibres reach the musculoskeletal system?","Back into the spinal nerve"],["","What is the route of sympathetic fibres from the spinal cord?","What is the route of sympathetic fibres from the spinal cord?","Nerve rootlets; Nerve root; Spinal nerve; Sympathetic trunk"]]},{"id":2090,"t":"Autonomic Nervous System","s":"Which statement about where sympathetic and parasympathetic nerves are located is correct?","o":["Sympathetic near the vertebral column; parasympathetic near target organs","Sympathetic near target organs; parasympathetic near the vertebral column","Both lie close to the vertebral column","Both lie close to the target organs","Sympathetic near the brainstem; parasympathetic near the sacrum"],"e":"<b>Sympathetic</b> nerves are located <b>close to the vertebral column</b>; <b>parasympathetic</b> nerves are located <b>close to target organs</b>.","w":["Reversed.","Parasympathetic lie near organs.","Sympathetic lie near the column.","That is outflow, not location relative to targets."],"k":"Sympathetic near spine; parasympathetic near organs.","rn":[["","Where are sympathetic nerves located?","Where are sympathetic nerves located?","Close to target organs"],["","Where are parasympathetic nerves located?","Where are parasympathetic nerves located?","Close to the vertebral column"]]},{"id":2091,"t":"Spinal Nerves","s":"A student says all spinal nerves have ganglia on their ventral roots. Which correction is right?","o":["The ganglion is on the dorsal root, which is afferent","The ganglion is on the dorsal root, which is efferent","The ganglion is on the ventral root, which is afferent","The ganglion is on the ventral ramus, which is efferent","There are no ganglia on spinal nerve roots"],"e":"The <b>dorsal root ganglion</b> sits on the <b>dorsal root</b>, which is <b>afferent (somatosensory)</b>. The ventral root is efferent.","w":["The dorsal root is afferent.","It is on the dorsal root.","It is on the dorsal root.","There is a dorsal root ganglion."],"k":"DRG on dorsal (afferent) root.","rn":[["","Dorsal Root","Dorsal Root","Afferent; Somatosensory; Incoming information"],["","Dorsal Root Ganglion","Dorsal Root Ganglion","A cluster of cell bodies; On the dorsal root"],["","How can you identify the dorsal root on an unlabelled diagram?","How can you identify the dorsal root on an unlabelled diagram?","It has the dorsal root ganglion"]]},{"id":2092,"t":"Spinal Nerves","s":"How many pairs of spinal nerves arise from the lumbar and sacral regions combined?","o":["10","5","12","17","8"],"e":"<b>5 lumbar + 5 sacral = 10</b> pairs. Full breakdown: 8 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral, 1 coccygeal = 31.","w":["Only one region.","Thoracic count.","Lumbar + thoracic.","Cervical count."],"k":"5 lumbar + 5 sacral.","rn":[["","What are the 5 groups of spinal nerves?","What are the 5 groups of spinal nerves?","Cervical; Thoracic; Lumbar; Sacral; Coccygeal"]]},{"id":2093,"t":"Spinal Nerves","s":"Which statement about the C8 spinal nerve is correct?","o":["It is the first spinal nerve to exit below its vertebra","It is the last spinal nerve to exit above its vertebra","It exits between the skull and the atlas","It is the last cervical nerve to exit above C7","It is the first spinal nerve to form the cauda equina"],"e":"Only <b>C1–C7 exit above</b> their vertebrae; <b>C8 onwards exit below</b>. There are 8 cervical nerves but only 7 cervical vertebrae.","w":["C7 is the last to exit above.","That is C1.","C8 exits below.","The cauda equina starts at L2."],"k":"C8 is the first to exit below.","rn":[["","Which spinal nerves exit below their vertebrae?","Which spinal nerves exit below their vertebrae?","C8 onwards (C8+)"]]},{"id":2094,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","s":"Which effect is <b>not</b> produced by sympathetic activation?","o":["Increased urination","Increased metabolism","Dilation of the airways","Mobilisation of glucose and fats","Increased respiratory functioning"],"e":"<b>Urination</b> is a <b>parasympathetic</b> (rest and digest) effect, mediated by the sacral outflow to the bladder. The others are sympathetic.","w":["Sympathetic.","Sympathetic.","Sympathetic.","Sympathetic."],"k":"Urination = parasympathetic.","rn":[["","What are the 3 functions of the sacral parasympathetic?","What are the 3 functions of the sacral parasympathetic?","Bladder: urination; Rectum: defecation; Sexual function"],["","Compare sympathetic vs parasympathetic effect on the heart","Compare sympathetic vs parasympathetic effect on the heart","Sympathetic: increases cardiac functioning; Parasympathetic: decreases HR"],["","Parasympathetic Function","Parasympathetic Function","Switched on during resting; Rest and digest; Increases/conserves metabolic resources"]]},{"id":2095,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","s":"A sympathetic preganglionic axon is traced. Which statement about its course is always true?","o":["It enters the sympathetic trunk","It synapses in the trunk at the same level","It synapses in a ganglion inside the target organ","It travels in a cranial nerve","It leaves the cord via the dorsal root"],"e":"<b>All preganglionic sympathetic axons enter the sympathetic trunk</b>. From there, they may synapse at the same level, travel up or down, or pass through without synapsing into a splanchnic nerve.","w":["It may synapse at other levels or not at all in the trunk.","That is parasympathetic.","Parasympathetic (brainstem).","Efferent fibres use the ventral root."],"k":"All preganglionic sympathetic axons enter the trunk.","rn":[["","Which axons enter the sympathetic trunk?","Which axons enter the sympathetic trunk?","All preganglionic sympathetic axons"]]},{"id":2096,"t":"Spinal Nerves","s":"Dorsal and ventral roots join at <b>B</b>, which is therefore a “mixed” nerve. Name it.","e":"B is the spinal nerve, formed where dorsal and ventral roots join; it is mixed (sensory, motor and sympathetic), is short, leaves through the intervertebral foramen and divides into dorsal and ventral primary rami.","k":"B = spinal nerve.","img":{"src":"2/roots","cap":"Transverse section of the spinal cord with its roots and nerves. Labels removed."},"a":["spinal nerve","mixed spinal nerve","spinal nerves"],"rn":[["","What is the full route from spinal cord to primary rami?","What is the full route from spinal cord to primary rami?","Nerve rootlets; Nerve roots (with DRG); Spinal nerve; Dorsal and ventral primary rami"],["","What 3 types of fibre does a spinal nerve contain?","What 3 types of fibre does a spinal nerve contain?","Motor; Sensory; Sympathetic; So they are mixed"],["","How long is a spinal nerve?","How long is a spinal nerve?","Very short"]]},{"id":2097,"t":"Spinal Nerves","s":"Name the thin strand labelled <b>E</b>, continuing down from the tip of the spinal cord in the midline.","e":"E is the filum terminale. D is the cut sacrum; C marks dorsal root ganglia of the lumbar nerves.","k":"E = filum terminale.","img":{"src":"2/cauda","cap":"Lower vertebral canal opened from behind (dissection). Labels removed."},"a":["filum terminale","filum"],"rn":[["","What 6 structures are seen in a cauda equina dissection?","What 6 structures are seen in a cauda equina dissection?","Cauda equina; Dura mater; Dorsal root ganglia of L2 and L3; 1st sacral nerve root; Sacrum (cut); Filum terminale"]]},{"id":2098,"t":"Autonomic Nervous System","s":"Which statement about parasympathetic branching is correct?","o":["Very little branching, so it cannot affect many organs rapidly","Plentiful branching, so many organs are affected simultaneously","Very little branching, so it affects many organs simultaneously","Plentiful branching, but only within the sympathetic trunk","No branching, because it has no ganglia"],"e":"Parasympathetic nerves have <b>very little branching</b>, so the division is <b>unable to affect multiple organs rapidly</b>.","w":["That is sympathetic.","Little branching means it cannot affect many at once.","Parasympathetic does not use the trunk.","It has ganglia near organs."],"k":"Parasympathetic: little branching.","rn":[["","How much branching does the parasympathetic division have?","How much branching does the parasympathetic division have?","Very little"],["","What does the map of the ANS show about the parasympathetic fibres?","What does the map of the ANS show about the parasympathetic fibres?","Really long preganglionic axons; Synapse very close to target organs; Little branching"]]},{"id":2099,"t":"Spinal Nerves","f":"nt24","s":"A section through a lumbar vertebra shows a spinal nerve dividing into a small posterior branch and a large anterior branch. Which statement is correct?","o":["Posterior is the dorsal primary ramus; anterior is the ventral primary ramus","Posterior is the ventral primary ramus; anterior is the dorsal primary ramus","Posterior is the dorsal root; anterior is the ventral root","Posterior is the dorsal primary ramus; anterior is the sympathetic trunk","Posterior is the ventral root; anterior is the dorsal root"],"e":"A spinal nerve divides into a <b>dorsal (posterior) primary ramus</b>, which is smaller, and a <b>ventral (anterior) primary ramus</b>, which is much larger.","w":["Reversed.","Roots are proximal to the spinal nerve; rami are branches.","The ventral ramus is the larger anterior branch.","Roots, and reversed."],"k":"Dorsal (posterior) ramus small; ventral (anterior) ramus large.","rn":[["","What 2 branches does a spinal nerve divide into?","What 2 branches does a spinal nerve divide into?","Dorsal/posterior primary ramus; Ventral/anterior primary ramus"]]},{"id":2100,"t":"Spinal Nerves","s":"Which statement about the somatic efferent supply to skeletal muscle and the ventral root is correct?","o":["Somatic motor axons leave the cord through the ventral root","Somatic motor axons leave the cord through the dorsal root","Somatic motor axons synapse in the dorsal root ganglion","Somatic motor axons synapse in the sympathetic trunk","Somatic motor axons synapse in a prevertebral ganglion"],"e":"The <b>ventral root</b> is <b>efferent</b>, carrying <b>somatic motor</b> (and sympathetic) axons. The somatic pathway is one motor neuron with no ganglion.","w":["The dorsal root is afferent.","The DRG is sensory.","Somatic pathways have no ganglion.","Somatic pathways have no ganglion."],"k":"Somatic motor axons exit via the ventral root.","rn":[["","Ventral Root","Ventral Root","Efferent; Somatic motor/sympathetic; Messages away from the CNS"]]},{"id":2101,"t":"Spinal Nerves","s":"Which pair matches each nerve group with the main region it supplies?","o":["Cervical – upper limb/head/neck; lumbar – pelvis/lower limb","Cervical – thorax/abdomen; lumbar – pelvis/lower limb","Cervical – upper limb/head/neck; lumbar – thorax/abdomen","Thoracic – upper limb/head/neck; sacral – thorax/abdomen","Thoracic – pelvis/lower limb; cervical – thorax/abdomen"],"e":"<b>Cervical</b>: upper limb/head/neck; <b>thoracic</b>: thorax/abdomen; <b>lumbar</b>: pelvis/lower limb; <b>sacral</b>: lower limb.","w":["Thorax/abdomen is thoracic.","Lumbar is pelvis/lower limb.","Thoracic is thorax/abdomen.","Mismatched."],"k":"Spinal nerve groups and regions supplied.","rn":[["","What do the cervical spinal nerves supply?","What do the cervical spinal nerves supply?","Upper limb; Head; Neck"],["","What do the lumbar spinal nerves supply?","What do the lumbar spinal nerves supply?","Pelvis; Lower limb"]]},{"id":2102,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","s":"A preganglionic parasympathetic axon from the sacral cord is traced. Where does it synapse?","o":["On or near the pelvic organs","In the sympathetic trunk","In a prevertebral ganglion in the abdomen","In the dorsal root ganglion","In the brainstem"],"e":"Sacral preganglionic axons exit via the <b>pelvic splanchnic nerves</b> and synapse with postganglionic axons <b>on or near the pelvic organs</b>.","w":["Sympathetic.","Sympathetic.","Sensory.","Not correct."],"k":"Sacral parasympathetic synapse on/near pelvic organs.","rn":[["","Where do sacral parasympathetic axons synapse?","Where do sacral parasympathetic axons synapse?","With postganglionic axons on/in proximity to the pelvic organs; Or embedded in their wall"],["","What does the sacral parasympathetic pathway supply?","What does the sacral parasympathetic pathway supply?","Pelvic organs only"]]},{"id":2103,"t":"Dermatomes and Myotome​","s":"Which pair of findings shows the clinical use of dermatomes?","o":["Umbilical numbness (T10) and diaphragm irritation felt in the shoulder (C3–C5)","Umbilical numbness (T12) and diaphragm irritation felt in the shoulder (C3–C5)","Umbilical numbness (T10) and diaphragm irritation felt in the shoulder (C5–C7)","Hand numbness (C8) and diaphragm irritation felt in the shoulder (C3–C5)","Umbilical numbness (T10) and kidney pain felt in the back (T12)"],"e":"The lecture's examples: loss of sensation over the <b>umbilicus</b> may indicate damage to <b>T10</b>; <b>diaphragmatic irritation</b> is referred to the <b>shoulder (C3–C5)</b>.","w":["The umbilicus is T10.","Shoulder referral is C3–C5.","The hand example was not used.","Kidney pain was not the example."],"k":"Lecture examples: T10 umbilicus; diaphragm → shoulder (C3–C5).","rn":[["","What is the example of referred pain?","What is the example of referred pain?","Diaphragmatic irritation; Referred to shoulder; C3 to C5 dermatomes"]]},{"id":2104,"t":"Spinal Nerves","s":"Which statement about the somatic vs autonomic contributions of a typical spinal nerve is correct?","o":["Somatic motor, somatosensory and sympathetic fibres","Somatic motor, somatosensory and parasympathetic fibres","Somatic motor and somatosensory fibres only","Somatosensory and sympathetic fibres only","Somatic motor and sympathetic fibres only"],"e":"Spinal nerves are <b>mixed</b>: somatic motor, somatosensory and <b>sympathetic</b> (sympathetic supply to the whole body). Parasympathetic outflow comes only from the brainstem and <b>sacral</b> cord.","w":["Parasympathetic outflow is cranial/sacral only.","Also sympathetic.","Also somatic motor.","Also somatosensory."],"k":"Spinal nerves: motor + sensory + sympathetic.","rn":[["","What fibres do both primary rami carry?","What fibres do both primary rami carry?","Somatic motor; Somatosensory; Sympathetic"]]},{"id":2105,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","s":"Which of the following correctly matches a pathway with its number of neurons from CNS to effector?","o":["Adrenal medulla – one; somatic – one; bladder (parasympathetic) – two","Adrenal medulla – two; somatic – one; bladder (parasympathetic) – two","Adrenal medulla – one; somatic – two; bladder (parasympathetic) – two","Adrenal medulla – one; somatic – one; bladder (parasympathetic) – one","Adrenal medulla – two; somatic – two; bladder (parasympathetic) – one"],"e":"The adrenal medulla receives a <b>single preganglionic axon</b>; the somatic pathway is <b>one motor neuron</b>; typical autonomic pathways (e.g. to the bladder) use <b>two neurons</b>.","w":["The adrenal medulla gets a single preganglionic axon.","Somatic is one motor neuron.","Typical autonomic pathways use two.","All wrong."],"k":"Count the neurons: adrenal 1, somatic 1, typical autonomic 2.","rn":[["","How is the adrenal medulla supplied?","How is the adrenal medulla supplied?","A single preganglionic axon only; Monosynaptic pathway"],["","What is the exception to the two-neuron rule?","What is the exception to the two-neuron rule?","Sympathetic supply to the adrenal medulla"],["","Which cells does the preganglionic axon innervate in the adrenal medulla?","Which cells does the preganglionic axon innervate in the adrenal medulla?","Chromaffin cells"]]},{"id":2106,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","s":"Which structure is described as “the motorway” of the sympathetic system?","o":["Sympathetic trunk","Spinal cord","Cauda equina","Vagus nerve","Pelvic splanchnic nerve"],"e":"Think of the <b>sympathetic trunk</b> as the <b>motorway (highway)</b>: axons join it, travel up or down, and leave at the right exit to reach their organ.","w":["Not the analogy.","Not the analogy.","Not discussed in this lecture.","Parasympathetic."],"k":"Sympathetic trunk = motorway.","rn":[["","Which axons enter the sympathetic trunk?","Which axons enter the sympathetic trunk?","All preganglionic sympathetic axons"],["","What are the 3 names for the sympathetic trunk?","What are the 3 names for the sympathetic trunk?","Sympathetic trunk; Sympathetic cord; Gangliated cord"],["","Where is the sympathetic trunk?","Where is the sympathetic trunk?","Bilateral to the spinal cord/vertebral column"]]},{"id":2107,"t":"Spinal Nerves","s":"Name the swelling labelled <b>D</b>, which contains the cell bodies of sensory neurons.","e":"D is the dorsal root ganglion: a cluster of pseudo-unipolar sensory cell bodies on the dorsal root.","k":"D = dorsal root ganglion.","img":{"src":"2/roots","cap":"Transverse section of the spinal cord with its roots and nerves. Labels removed."},"a":["dorsal root ganglion","drg","dorsal root ganglia","spinal ganglion","posterior root ganglion"],"rn":[["","Dorsal Root Ganglion","Dorsal Root Ganglion","A cluster of cell bodies; On the dorsal root"]]},{"id":2108,"t":"Autonomic Nervous System","s":"A person under threat has dilated vessels in skeletal muscle and constricted vessels in the kidneys. Why is this pattern useful?","o":["It sends blood to essential tissues, maximising resources for fight or flight","It sends blood to essential tissues, conserving resources for later use","It sends blood to the kidneys, promoting urination under threat","It sends blood to the gut, promoting digestion under threat","It sends blood away from muscle, slowing the heart to save energy"],"e":"Sympathetic activity <b>maximises use of resources</b>: it dilates vessels to muscles, heart, liver and adipose tissue and <b>constricts vessels to non-essential organs</b> such as the kidneys and GI tract.","w":["Conserving is parasympathetic.","Kidney vessels constrict.","GI vessels constrict.","Vessels to muscle dilate."],"k":"Sympathetic redistributes blood to essential tissues.","rn":[["","Vessels to which organs constrict in fight or flight?","Vessels to which organs constrict in fight or flight?","Non-essential organs; Kidneys; GI tract"],["","Sympathetic Function","Sympathetic Function","Fight or flight response; Maximises use of metabolic resources"],["","Vessels to which 4 tissues dilate in fight or flight?","Vessels to which 4 tissues dilate in fight or flight?","Muscles; Cardiac muscle; Liver; Adipose tissue"]]},{"id":2109,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","s":"Which pair of ganglion types is associated with the sympathetic division?","o":["Paravertebral and prevertebral","Dorsal root and prevertebral","Paravertebral and dorsal root","Intramural (in the organ) and paravertebral","Prevertebral and intramural (in the organ)"],"e":"Sympathetic ganglia are <b>paravertebral</b> (forming the sympathetic trunk) or <b>prevertebral</b> (reached via splanchnic nerves).","w":["The DRG is sensory.","The DRG is sensory.","Ganglia in organs are parasympathetic.","Ganglia in organs are parasympathetic."],"k":"Sympathetic ganglia: paravertebral + prevertebral.","rn":[["","Paravertebral vs prevertebral ganglia","Paravertebral vs prevertebral ganglia","Paravertebral: form the sympathetic trunk; Prevertebral: reached by splanchnic nerves"]]},{"id":2110,"t":"Spinal Nerves","s":"Name the tough membrane labelled <b>B</b>, opened to expose the nerve roots.","e":"B is the dura mater, the outer meningeal layer enclosing the cauda equina.","k":"B = dura mater.","img":{"src":"2/cauda","cap":"Lower vertebral canal opened from behind (dissection). Labels removed."},"a":["dura mater","dura","dural sac","thecal sac"],"rn":[["","What 6 structures are seen in a cauda equina dissection?","What 6 structures are seen in a cauda equina dissection?","Cauda equina; Dura mater; Dorsal root ganglia of L2 and L3; 1st sacral nerve root; Sacrum (cut); Filum terminale"]]},{"id":2112,"t":"Dermatomes and Myotome​","s":"A patient with spinal cord damage at the T10 level is examined. Which finding is most consistent with this level?","o":["Altered sensation in a band of skin around the umbilicus","Altered sensation over the tip of the shoulder","Altered sensation in a band just below the nipples","Altered sensation over the groin","Altered sensation limited to the hand"],"e":"<b>T10 = umbilicus</b>. Loss/altered sensation in that dermatome suggests injury to the spinal cord, nerve root or spinal nerve at T10.","w":["Shoulder is C3–C5 referral.","T10 is at the umbilicus.","Not T10.","Not T10."],"k":"T10 = umbilicus.","rn":[["","What does loss/altered sensation or pain from a dermatome indicate?","What does loss/altered sensation or pain from a dermatome indicate?","Possible injury to the spinal cord; Nerve root (or rootlets); Or spinal nerve"]]},{"id":2113,"t":"Autonomic Nervous System","s":"Which of the following best describes what “involuntary” means for the ANS?","o":["We don’t have to think about it; it just happens","It works only while we are asleep","It can be switched on only by drugs","It is controlled by the cerebellum","It works only when skeletal muscle is relaxed"],"e":"<b>Involuntary</b> means <b>we don't have to think about it</b>; it just happens. The ANS optimises performance as a survival mechanism and maintains the internal environment.","w":["It works all the time.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Involuntary = happens without thinking.","rn":[["","What does involuntary mean?","What does involuntary mean?","We don't have to think about it; It just happens"]]},{"id":2114,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","s":"From which part of the spinal cord do sympathetic preganglionic axons leave, and by which route do they reach the trunk?","o":["Lateral/ventral cord, through the spinal nerve into the trunk","Dorsal cord, through the dorsal root ganglion into the trunk","Brainstem, through the cranial nerves to the trunk","Sacral cord, through the pelvic splanchnic nerves","Lateral/ventral cord, directly to the target organ"],"e":"From the spinal cord (<b>lateral/ventral</b>), sympathetic fibres pass through the <b>spinal nerve</b> into the <b>sympathetic trunk</b>.","w":["Sympathetic axons leave ventrally.","Brainstem outflow is parasympathetic.","That is parasympathetic.","All preganglionic sympathetic axons enter the trunk."],"k":"Sympathetic: cord (lateral/ventral) → spinal nerve → trunk.","rn":[["","What is the route of sympathetic fibres from the spinal cord?","What is the route of sympathetic fibres from the spinal cord?","Nerve rootlets; Nerve root; Spinal nerve; Sympathetic trunk"],["","Through what do sympathetic fibres move from the spinal nerve into the trunk?","Through what do sympathetic fibres move from the spinal nerve into the trunk?","Rami communicantes"],["","What is the full route from spinal cord to primary rami?","What is the full route from spinal cord to primary rami?","Nerve rootlets; Nerve roots (with DRG); Spinal nerve; Dorsal and ventral primary rami"]]},{"id":2115,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","s":"Which statement about the thoracolumbar and craniosacral outflows is correct?","o":["Thoracolumbar is sympathetic; craniosacral is parasympathetic","Thoracolumbar is parasympathetic; craniosacral is sympathetic","Both thoracolumbar and craniosacral are sympathetic","Both thoracolumbar and craniosacral are parasympathetic","Thoracolumbar is somatic; craniosacral is autonomic"],"e":"<b>Thoracolumbar = sympathetic</b>: fight or flight, maximises resources. <b>Craniosacral = parasympathetic</b>: rest and digest, conserves resources.","w":["Reversed.","Craniosacral is parasympathetic.","Thoracolumbar is sympathetic.","Both are autonomic."],"k":"Thoracolumbar = sympathetic; craniosacral = parasympathetic.","rn":[["","How do you remember the ANS origins?","How do you remember the ANS origins?","Para goes to the ends: head and tail; MNEMONIC"],["","Craniosacral","Craniosacral","Parasympathetic"],["","Thoracolumbar","Thoracolumbar","Sympathetic"]]},{"id":2116,"t":"Dermatomes and Myotome​","s":"Which statement about a myotome and a dermatome supplied by the same spinal nerve is correct?","o":["Myotome = its muscles; dermatome = its area of skin","Myotome = its area of skin; dermatome = its muscles","Both = its area of skin","Both = its muscles","Myotome = its ganglion; dermatome = its skin"],"e":"For one spinal nerve: <b>dermatome</b> = area of <b>skin</b>; <b>myotome</b> = set of <b>muscles</b>. Both result from segmental organisation.","w":["Reversed.","Myotome is muscle.","Dermatome is skin.","Myotome is muscle."],"k":"Dermatome = skin; myotome = muscle.","rn":[["","Dermatome vs myotome","Dermatome vs myotome","Dermatome: area of skin; Myotome: set of muscles; Both: single spinal nerve"]]},{"id":2117,"t":"Autonomic Nervous System","s":"In what sense is the ANS a “survival mechanism”?","o":["Optimising performance and the internal environment","Learning and storing new motor skills","Detecting light and controlling eye movement during reading","Myelinating the peripheral nerves","Forming new memories during sleep"],"e":"The ANS <b>optimises our performance</b> as a <b>survival mechanism</b> and <b>maintains an adequate internal environment</b> for cells and tissues to work.","w":["Not the ANS role.","Not the ANS survival role described.","Myelin is made by glia.","Memory is hippocampal."],"k":"ANS optimises performance and maintains the internal environment.","rn":[["","Why is the ANS a survival mechanism?","Why is the ANS a survival mechanism?","It optimises our performance and survival; Maintains an adequate internal environment for cells and tissues"]]},{"id":2118,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","s":"Which is the best description of the sympathetic supply route to the heart and lungs in the pathway summary?","o":["From the cord into the sympathetic trunk, then to the heart, lungs or head","From the cord into the trunk, then via pelvic splanchnics to the heart","From the cord by a single preganglionic axon, as for the adrenal medulla","From the brainstem via cranial nerves to the heart and lungs","From the cord via the dorsal primary ramus to the heart and lungs"],"e":"In the summary of sympathetic pathways, fibres pass from the cord through the spinal nerve into the <b>sympathetic trunk</b>, and one route continues <b>to the heart, lungs or head</b>.","w":["Pelvic splanchnics are sacral parasympathetic.","Only the adrenal medulla has this.","That is parasympathetic.","Dorsal rami supply the back."],"k":"Sympathetic to heart/lungs/head via the trunk.","rn":[["","What are the 3 destinations in the summary of sympathetic pathways?","What are the 3 destinations in the summary of sympathetic pathways?","To musculoskeletal system; To heart, lungs or head; To abdomen"],["","What does the brainstem parasympathetic pathway supply?","What does the brainstem parasympathetic pathway supply?","Head; Heart; Lungs; Abdomen"]]},{"id":2119,"t":"Dermatomes and Myotome​","s":"What is the significance of the dorsal root being “afferent” for a patient with a lesion of that root?","o":["Sensation from the matching dermatome is lost or altered","Muscles of the matching myotome would be paralysed or weakened","Sympathetic supply to that segment would be lost","Parasympathetic supply to the pelvis would be lost","Sensation from the whole limb would be lost"],"e":"The <b>dorsal root</b> is <b>afferent (somatosensory)</b>; a dermatome is the area of skin responsible for sensory input to the dorsal root. A lesion causes <b>loss/altered sensation</b> in that dermatome.","w":["Motor fibres are in the ventral root.","Sympathetic fibres leave via the ventral root.","Parasympathetic is sacral outflow.","One root = one dermatome."],"k":"Dorsal root lesion → dermatomal sensory loss.","rn":[["","What does loss/altered sensation or pain from a dermatome indicate?","What does loss/altered sensation or pain from a dermatome indicate?","Possible injury to the spinal cord; Nerve root (or rootlets); Or spinal nerve"]]},{"id":2120,"t":"Autonomic Nervous System","s":"A patient describes a strong fight-or-flight response to something most people would find harmless. Which approach is most appropriate?","o":["If someone has a fight-or-flight response, don’t minimise it","A threat counts only if most people would perceive it as one","The parasympathetic division is what detects the threat","Fight or flight is triggered by the same stimuli in every person","Freeze is the best-evidenced part of the stress response"],"e":"What counts as a threat is <b>individual</b>, so it is not for us to judge: if someone has initiated a fight-or-flight response, <b>do not minimise their stress</b>.","w":["Threat is individual.","Not stated.","Threat is individual.","No evidence for freeze."],"k":"Threat perception is individual; don't minimise stress.","rn":[["","What must exist before a fight or flight response?","What must exist before a fight or flight response?","A mechanism that identifies the threat"],["","Is fight or flight about an imminent threat?","Is fight or flight about an imminent threat?","No, the perception of threat; It is an individual experience"],["","Adrenaline release is one of the initiators of which response?","Adrenaline release is one of the initiators of which response?","Fight or flight"]]},{"id":2121,"t":"Autonomic Nervous System","s":"A 22-year-old student is walking home at night when a large dog suddenly runs at her, barking. Within seconds her pupils widen, her heart pounds and she breathes faster. Her mouth feels dry and she has no urge to eat or go to the toilet. Blood tests taken in a research study at the time show raised glucose and fats.<br><br>Which division is active, and what is happening to blood flow to her kidneys and gut?","o":["Sympathetic; vessels to kidneys and GI tract constrict","Parasympathetic; vessels to kidneys and GI tract dilate","Sympathetic; vessels to kidneys and GI tract dilate to mobilise nutrients from the gut quickly","Parasympathetic; vessels to all organs constrict","Somatic; vessels to skeletal muscle constrict"],"e":"Step 1: dilated pupils, raised heart and breathing rate and mobilised glucose and fats are \"fight or flight\" effects of the sympathetic division, which maximises use of metabolic resources. Step 2: the sympathetic division dilates vessels to muscles, heart, liver and adipose tissue but constricts vessels to non-essential organs such as the kidneys and GI (gastrointestinal) tract.","w":["Parasympathetic is \"rest and digest\" and would slow the heart and narrow the pupils.","Sympathetic activity constricts, not dilates, vessels to non-essential organs.","Her signs are sympathetic, and parasympathetic activity does not constrict all vessels.","The somatic system controls skeletal muscle voluntarily, not blood vessels."],"k":"Sympathetic: dilates muscle vessels, constricts kidney/GI vessels.","rn":[["","Vessels to which organs constrict in fight or flight?","Vessels to which organs constrict in fight or flight?","Non-essential organs; Kidneys; GI tract"]]},{"id":2122,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","s":"A 60-year-old man has a large meal and sits down to rest. His heart rate slows, his pupils narrow, and he produces more saliva. Shortly afterwards he needs to open his bowels. His doctor explains that the nerves causing this have their ganglia very close to the target organs.<br><br>Which division is active, and what does the position of its ganglia mean for its axons?","o":["Parasympathetic; long preganglionic and short postganglionic axons","Sympathetic; short preganglionic and long postganglionic axons","Parasympathetic; short preganglionic and long postganglionic axons","Sympathetic; ganglia in the sympathetic trunk along the vertebral column","Somatic; one motor neuron runs straight from the spinal cord to the organ with no ganglion on the way"],"e":"Step 1: salivation, defecation and decreased heart rate and pupil diameter are \"rest and digest\" effects of the parasympathetic division. Step 2: parasympathetic ganglia lie close to (or in) the target organ, so the preganglionic axon must travel a long way and the postganglionic axon is short.","w":["These are not sympathetic effects, and sympathetic ganglia lie near the spine.","Ganglia near the organ give long pre- and short postganglionic axons, not the reverse.","Sympathetic trunk ganglia are near the spine and the effects here are parasympathetic.","The somatic system controls skeletal muscle, not gut or pupils."],"k":"Parasympathetic ganglia near organ: long pre, short post.","rn":[["","What is the primary anatomical difference of the parasympathetic division?","What is the primary anatomical difference of the parasympathetic division?","Really long preganglionic axons; Really short postganglionic axons"]]},{"id":2123,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","s":"A pharmacology student studies a drug that blocks transmission at autonomic ganglia. When given to an animal, both the sympathetic and parasympathetic effects on the heart are reduced. However, the release of adrenaline from the adrenal medulla is affected differently from the other sympathetic targets.<br><br>Which statement best explains these findings?","o":["Ganglia use acetylcholine; the adrenal medulla is supplied by a single preganglionic axon","Ganglia use adrenaline; the adrenal medulla has two postganglionic neurons","Only sympathetic ganglia use acetylcholine, so the parasympathetic heart effects should not change","The adrenal medulla is supplied by the parasympathetic sacral outflow","The heart is a somatic effector supplied by one motor neuron"],"e":"Step 1: the ANS usually uses two neurons: a preganglionic neuron from the CNS synapses in an autonomic ganglion, using acetylcholine, with a postganglionic neuron to the effector, so blocking the ganglion reduces both divisions. Step 2: the exception is the sympathetic supply to the adrenal medulla, which is a single preganglionic axon with no postganglionic neuron, so it behaves differently.","w":["The ganglionic neurotransmitter is acetylcholine.","Both divisions synapse in ganglia using acetylcholine.","The adrenal medulla has a sympathetic supply.","Cardiac muscle is an autonomic effector."],"k":"Ganglia use ACh; adrenal medulla = single preganglionic axon.","rn":[["","How is the adrenal medulla supplied?","How is the adrenal medulla supplied?","A single preganglionic axon only; Monosynaptic pathway"]]},{"id":2124,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","s":"A 55-year-old man has surgery in the pelvis. Afterwards he cannot pass urine normally, has difficulty with defecation and reports sexual dysfunction. His upper body autonomic functions, including heart rate and digestion higher in the gut, are normal. The surgeon suspects damage to nerves carrying fibres from the lowest part of the spinal cord.<br><br>Which nerves are most likely damaged, and which division do they belong to?","o":["Pelvic splanchnic nerves; sacral parasympathetic","Sympathetic trunk; thoracolumbar sympathetic","Cranial nerves; brainstem parasympathetic","Intercostal nerves (T1–T11); somatic","Dorsal primary rami; supplying the back"],"e":"Step 1: bladder (urination), rectum (defecation) and reproductive organs (sexual function) are the pelvic organs supplied by the sacral parasympathetic outflow. Step 2: sacral preganglionic axons exit via the pelvic splanchnic nerves and synapse on or near the pelvic organs; the brainstem parasympathetic supply to the head, heart, lungs and abdomen is spared, so upper functions are normal.","w":["The sympathetic trunk is a paravertebral chain, not the pelvic parasympathetic route.","Cranial nerves carry the brainstem outflow, which is intact here.","Intercostal nerves supply the chest wall.","Dorsal rami supply the back, not the pelvic organs."],"k":"Pelvic splanchnic nerves = sacral parasympathetic to bladder, rectum, sex organs.","rn":[["","What are the 2 parasympathetic pathways?","What are the 2 parasympathetic pathways?","Brainstem via cranial nerves; Sacral spinal cord via pelvic splanchnic nerves"]]},{"id":2125,"t":"Spinal Nerves","s":"A 40-year-old woman has a disc problem that compresses one nerve root inside her vertebral canal. She has lost sensation in a strip of skin but has full muscle strength in the same area. An MRI shows that the root affected carries a swelling, the ganglion, along its length.<br><br>Which root is compressed, and which type of fibre does it carry?","o":["Dorsal root; afferent (somatosensory) fibres","Ventral root; efferent (somatic motor and sympathetic) fibres","Dorsal root; efferent motor fibres to skeletal muscle","Ventral root; afferent fibres with the dorsal root ganglion","Ventral primary ramus; carrying only autonomic fibres to the organs of the pelvis and lower limb"],"e":"Step 1: loss of sensation with normal strength points to a sensory (afferent) problem, and the root with a ganglion is the dorsal root (the dorsal root ganglion is a cluster of sensory cell bodies). Step 2: the dorsal root is afferent (somatosensory), while the ventral root is efferent (somatic motor and sympathetic), which is why strength is spared.","w":["A ventral root lesion would cause weakness, and it has no ganglion.","The dorsal root is afferent, not motor.","The dorsal root ganglion sits on the dorsal root, not the ventral root.","Primary rami lie outside the canal and carry motor, sensory and sympathetic fibres."],"k":"Dorsal root = afferent + DRG; ventral root = efferent.","rn":[["","Dorsal Root","Dorsal Root","Afferent; Somatosensory; Incoming information"]]},{"id":2126,"t":"Dermatomes and Myotome​","s":"A 48-year-old man falls from a ladder and injures his back. On examination he has lost sensation in a band of skin around his trunk at the level of the umbilicus. Sensation above and below this band is broadly normal. His doctor wants to localise the injury.<br><br>Which level is most likely injured, and what could the injury involve?","o":["T10; the spinal cord, nerve root or spinal nerve","C3–C5; the diaphragm, via referred pain","L1; the cauda equina only","T1; the dorsal primary ramus to the erector spinae","S1; a cranial nerve, because the umbilicus is supplied by the nerves of the brainstem"],"e":"Step 1: the dermatome at the umbilicus is T10 (a dermatome is an area of skin innervated by a single spinal nerve). Step 2: loss or altered sensation in a dermatome indicates possible injury to the spinal cord, nerve root or spinal nerve at that level, so dermatomes help locate the injury.","w":["C3–C5 is the shoulder dermatome linked to diaphragm referred pain.","L1 is not the umbilical dermatome.","T1 is not the umbilical dermatome.","The trunk is supplied by spinal nerves, not cranial nerves."],"k":"T10 = umbilicus; dermatome loss localises the lesion.","rn":[["","What does loss/altered sensation or pain from a dermatome indicate?","What does loss/altered sensation or pain from a dermatome indicate?","Possible injury to the spinal cord; Nerve root (or rootlets); Or spinal nerve"]]},{"id":2127,"t":"Dermatomes and Myotome​","s":"A 35-year-old woman has bleeding in her abdomen after an injury. The blood irritates the underside of her diaphragm. She complains mainly of pain at the tip of her shoulder, although the shoulder itself is not injured and moves normally.<br><br>Which mechanism explains her shoulder pain, and which spinal levels are involved?","o":["Referred visceral pain felt in the C3–C5 dermatomes","Direct injury to the T10 dermatome over the shoulder","A myotome injury of the intercostal muscles (T1–T11)","Sympathetic fight-or-flight pain sent to the shoulder from the adrenal medulla","Damage to the cauda equina at L2–L5"],"e":"Step 1: pain from an internal organ (visceral pain) can be referred to the skin of a dermatome and felt as cutaneous pain. Step 2: diaphragmatic irritation is referred to the shoulder, which is the C3–C5 dermatomes, so she feels shoulder-tip pain without a shoulder injury.","w":["T10 is the umbilicus, not the shoulder.","Myotomes are muscle groups, and the intercostals are not involved.","Referred pain is not a sympathetic or adrenal effect.","The cauda equina supplies the pelvis and lower limbs."],"k":"Diaphragm irritation → shoulder pain (C3–C5) = referred pain.","rn":[["","Referred Pain","Referred Pain","Visceral (organ) pain referred to the skin (dermatomes); Felt as cutaneous pain"],["","What is the example of referred pain?","What is the example of referred pain?","Diaphragmatic irritation; Referred to shoulder; C3 to C5 dermatomes"]]},{"id":2128,"t":"Spinal Nerves","s":"A 50-year-old man has a large disc prolapse in his lower back that compresses the bundle of nerve roots below the end of the spinal cord. He develops weakness and numbness in both legs and loses control of his anal sphincters. Imaging shows the compressed roots look like a horse's tail.<br><br>Which structure is compressed, and which roots does it contain?","o":["Cauda equina; L2–L5, S1–S5 and the coccygeal nerve roots","Sympathetic trunk; the paravertebral ganglia from T1 to L2","Brachial plexus; C5–T1 roots","Cauda equina; only the five sacral roots","Pelvic splanchnic nerves; parasympathetic fibres that supply only the bladder and rectum, not the legs"],"e":"Step 1: a bundle of nerve roots that looks like a horse's tail is the cauda equina (Latin for \"horse's tail\"). Step 2: it contains the roots of L2–L5, S1–S5 and the coccygeal nerve and innervates the pelvis, lower limbs and the internal/external anal sphincters, matching his leg and sphincter problems.","w":["The sympathetic trunk is a chain of ganglia, not a horse-tail bundle of roots.","The brachial plexus is not described in this lecture and serves the upper limb.","The cauda equina includes lumbar and coccygeal roots too.","Pelvic splanchnic nerves do not supply leg muscles or skin."],"k":"Cauda equina: L2–L5, S1–S5, Co → pelvis, legs, anal sphincters.","rn":[["","Cauda Equina","Cauda Equina","Latin for horse's tail; Bundle of spinal nerves and their roots; L2 to L5, S1 to S5 and the coccygeal nerve"]]},{"id":2129,"t":"Spinal Nerves","s":"A 30-year-old man is stabbed in the back just beside the spine. Afterwards he has numbness of the skin over the paravertebral gutter, weakness of his erector spinae muscles and pain from the facet joints at that level. The skin and muscles of the front of his trunk and his limbs are normal.<br><br>Which branch is injured, and why are the front of the trunk and limbs spared?","o":["Dorsal primary ramus; the ventral ramus supplies the rest of the body","Ventral primary ramus; the dorsal ramus supplies the limbs","Ventral root; it carries only sensory fibres to the back","Dorsal root; it carries the motor fibres to erector spinae","Sympathetic trunk; it carries somatic motor fibres"],"e":"Step 1: the dorsal (posterior) primary ramus supplies the skin over the paravertebral gutter, the erector spinae muscles and the facet joints of the vertebral column. Step 2: the much larger ventral (anterior) primary ramus supplies the rest of the body (skin and musculoskeletal system, except the head and parts of the neck), so the front and limbs are spared.","w":["The ventral ramus supplies the front and limbs, which are normal.","The ventral root is efferent (motor and sympathetic), not sensory.","The dorsal root is afferent (sensory), not motor.","The sympathetic trunk carries sympathetic, not somatic motor, fibres."],"k":"Dorsal ramus = back (skin, erector spinae, facets); ventral = rest.","rn":[["","What does the ventral primary ramus supply?","What does the ventral primary ramus supply?","Rest of the body; Except head/parts of neck; Skin and musculoskeletal system"]]},{"id":2130,"t":"Anatomy of the ANS","s":"A researcher compares two pathways in an animal. Pathway A runs from the spinal cord to a skeletal muscle with one neuron and no ganglion, and the animal can control it voluntarily. Pathway B runs from the spinal cord to the smooth muscle of a blood vessel and is not under voluntary control. A drug that blocks ganglia is given.<br><br>Which pathway is affected by the drug, and why?","o":["Pathway B, because autonomic pathways synapse in a ganglion","Pathway A, because somatic motor neurons synapse in a ganglion","Both, because all efferent pathways use a two-neuron chain","Neither, because smooth muscle is a somatic effector","Pathway A, because skeletal muscle is supplied by the dorsal root ganglion"],"e":"Step 1: pathway A (one motor neuron, voluntary, skeletal muscle) is somatic, and pathway B (involuntary, smooth muscle) is autonomic. Step 2: autonomic pathways usually use two neurons that synapse in an autonomic ganglion, whereas the somatic motor neuron runs straight to skeletal muscle with no ganglion, so only pathway B is blocked.","w":["Somatic motor neurons run directly to muscle without a ganglion.","Only autonomic pathways use the two-neuron chain.","Smooth muscle is an autonomic effector.","The dorsal root ganglion holds sensory cell bodies, not motor neurons."],"k":"Somatic: one neuron, no ganglion; autonomic: two neurons + ganglion.","rn":[["","Preganglionic Neuron","Preganglionic Neuron","From the CNS to an autonomic ganglion"],["","Autonomic Ganglia","Autonomic Ganglia","Where preganglionic neurons synapse with postganglionic neurons"],["","What is the neurotransmitter at the autonomic ganglion?","What is the neurotransmitter at the autonomic ganglion?","Acetylcholine"]]},{"id":2521,"t":"Dermatomes and Myotome​","s":"A patient has lost sensation over the strip labelled <b>A</b>, which runs around the trunk at the level of the umbilicus. Which spinal nerve supplies it?","a":["t10","t 10","tenth thoracic","t10 spinal nerve"],"e":"T10 = umbilicus. Loss of sensation there may indicate damage to the spinal cord, nerve root or spinal nerve at T10.","k":"T10 = umbilicus.","img":{"src":"2/o_derm","cap":"Dermatome map, anterior view (from Grant’s atlas). Image: Grant, John Charles Boileau, Public domain, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","T10","T10","Umbilicus"]]},{"id":2522,"t":"Dermatomes and Myotome​","s":"A patient with irritation of the diaphragm feels pain over area <b>B</b>, at the top of the shoulder. What explains this?","o":["Visceral pain referred to the C3–C5 dermatomes","Direct injury to the T10 spinal nerve","Pain referred to the C6 dermatome of the forearm","A lesion of the shoulder myotome only","Damage to the dorsal primary ramus of T5"],"w":["T10 supplies the umbilicus.","C6 is on the lateral forearm and thumb, not the shoulder tip.","A myotome is muscles, not skin sensation.","T5 is on the thorax."],"e":"Visceral (organ) pain can be referred to skin dermatomes as cutaneous pain: diaphragmatic irritation is felt at the shoulder (C3–C5 dermatomes).","k":"Diaphragm → shoulder (C3–C5).","img":{"src":"2/o_derm","cap":"Dermatome map, anterior view (from Grant’s atlas). Image: Grant, John Charles Boileau, Public domain, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","What is the example of referred pain?","What is the example of referred pain?","Diaphragmatic irritation; Referred to shoulder; C3 to C5 dermatomes"],["","Referred Pain","Referred Pain","Visceral (organ) pain referred to the skin (dermatomes); Felt as cutaneous pain"]]},{"id":2523,"t":"Dermatomes and Myotome​","s":"Which statement best defines the strips shown, such as A?","o":["An area of skin supplied by a single spinal nerve, carrying sensory input to its dorsal root","A set of muscles supplied by a single spinal nerve","An area of skin supplied by a single peripheral named nerve such as the median nerve","Part of the somite that develops into muscles","An area of skin whose blood supply comes from one intercostal artery"],"w":["That is a myotome.","Dermatomes are defined by spinal nerves (primary rami).","That is the embryological sense of myotome.","Dermatomes are about nerve supply, not arteries."],"e":"Dermatome (Greek derma, skin): an individual area of skin innervated by a single spinal nerve (primary ramus), the skin responsible for sensory input to that dorsal root.","k":"Dermatome = skin of one spinal nerve.","img":{"src":"2/o_derm","cap":"Dermatome map, anterior view (from Grant’s atlas). Image: Grant, John Charles Boileau, Public domain, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","Dermatome","Dermatome","Area of skin innervated by a single spinal nerve (primary ramus); Sensory input to the dorsal root"]]}]);
